1
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:33,669
Rosebud.

2
00:03:11,832 --> 00:03:14,001
News on the March.

3
00:03:33,520 --> 00:03:35,564
Legendary was the Xanadu...

4
00:03:35,647 --> 00:03:39,234
...where Kubla Khan decreed his stately pleasure dome.

5
00:03:39,318 --> 00:03:42,821
Today almost as legendary as Florida's Xanadu...

6
00:03:42,904 --> 00:03:45,240
...the world's largest private pleasure ground.

7
00:03:47,993 --> 00:03:51,288
Here on the deserts of the Gulf Coast, a private mountain...

8
00:03:51,371 --> 00:03:53,373
...was commissioned and successfully built.

9
00:03:53,415 --> 00:03:57,002
One hundred thousand trees, twenty thousand tons of marble...

10
00:03:57,085 --> 00:03:59,671
...are the ingredients of Xanadu's mountain.

11
00:04:00,756 --> 00:04:03,175
“上都”宮殿的藏品包括：
Contents of Xanadu抯 palace:

12
00:04:03,258 --> 00:04:07,429
Paintings, pictures, statues, various stones of other palaces.

13
00:04:07,804 --> 00:04:09,681
A collection of everything.

14
00:04:09,765 --> 00:04:13,018
So big it can never be catalogued or appraised.

15
00:04:13,101 --> 00:04:16,938
Enough for 10 museums, the loot of the world.

16
00:04:20,525 --> 00:04:22,486
Xanadu's livestock...

17
00:04:23,570 --> 00:04:29,076
...the fowl of the air, the fish of the sea, the beast of the field and jungle...

18
00:04:29,368 --> 00:04:33,622
...two of each, the biggest private zoo since Noah.

19
00:04:34,331 --> 00:04:36,124
Like the Pharaohs...

20
00:04:36,333 --> 00:04:40,253
...Xanadu's landlord leaves many stones to mark his grave.

21
00:04:41,797 --> 00:04:43,340
Since the Pyramids...

22
00:04:43,590 --> 00:04:46,301
...Xanadu is the costliest monument...

23
00:04:46,343 --> 00:04:48,804
...a man has built to himself.

24
00:05:01,858 --> 00:05:04,486
Here in Xanadu last week...

25
00:05:04,861 --> 00:05:07,739
...Xanadu's landlord was laid to rest.

26
00:05:08,073 --> 00:05:10,826
A potent figure of our century...

27
00:05:10,909 --> 00:05:13,078
...America's Kubla Khan:

28
00:05:13,537 --> 00:05:15,372
Charles Foster Kane.

29
00:05:42,858 --> 00:05:47,863
Its humble beginnings, in this ramshackle building, a dying daily.

30
00:05:47,988 --> 00:05:50,574
Kane's empire, in its glory...

31
00:05:50,741 --> 00:05:54,703
...held dominion over 37 newspapers, two syndicates...

32
00:05:54,953 --> 00:05:59,499
...a radio network, an empire upon an empire.

33
00:05:59,833 --> 00:06:03,712
The first of grocery stores, paper mills...

34
00:06:03,837 --> 00:06:09,509
...apartment buildings, factories, forests, ocean liners.

35
00:06:09,551 --> 00:06:12,929
An empire through which for 50 years...

36
00:06:13,013 --> 00:06:15,432
...flowed in an unending stream...

37
00:06:15,724 --> 00:06:18,935
...the wealth of the Earth's third richest gold mine.

38
00:06:20,312 --> 00:06:23,523
Famed in American legend is the origin of the Kane fortune.

39
00:06:23,565 --> 00:06:29,196
How, to boarding housekeeper Mary Kane, by a defaulting boarder, in 1868...

40
00:06:29,237 --> 00:06:33,116
...was left the supposedly worthless deed to an abandoned mineshaft:

41
00:06:33,200 --> 00:06:36,328
The Colorado Lode.

42
00:06:36,411 --> 00:06:40,582
Fifty-seven years later, before a congressional investigation...

43
00:06:40,624 --> 00:06:44,086
... Walter P. Thatcher, grand old man of Wall Street...

44
00:06:44,169 --> 00:06:47,923
...for years chief target of Kane papers' attacks on trusts...

45
00:06:47,964 --> 00:06:50,384
...recalls a journey he made as a youth.

46
00:06:50,467 --> 00:06:53,887
My firm had been appointed trustee by Mrs. Kane...

47
00:06:53,929 --> 00:06:56,264
...for a large fortune she recently acquired.

48
00:06:56,348 --> 00:07:00,435
It was her wish that I take charge of this boy, Charles Foster Kane.

49
00:07:00,519 --> 00:07:05,273
Chief, is it not, that on this occasion, Charles Foster Kane...

50
00:07:05,357 --> 00:07:10,195
...personally attacked you after striking you in the stomach with a sled?

51
00:07:10,737 --> 00:07:13,657
I shall read to the committee a prepared statement...

52
00:07:13,740 --> 00:07:19,204
...which I have brought with me, and then refuse to answer questions.

53
00:07:19,663 --> 00:07:23,041
Mr. Charles Foster Kane, in every essence of his social beliefs...

54
00:07:23,125 --> 00:07:26,086
...and by the dangerous manner he has persistently attacked...

55
00:07:26,169 --> 00:07:28,463
...American traditions of private property...

56
00:07:28,505 --> 00:07:30,882
...initiative and opportunity for advancement...

57
00:07:30,966 --> 00:07:34,845
...is, in fact, nothing more or less than a communist.

58
00:07:34,970 --> 00:07:37,431
That same month in Union Square....

59
00:07:37,514 --> 00:07:40,183
The words "Charles Foster Kane"...

60
00:07:40,267 --> 00:07:43,770
...are a menace to every workingman in this land.

61
00:07:44,104 --> 00:07:49,484
He is today what he has always been and always will be: a fascist.

62
00:07:49,901 --> 00:07:51,820
And still another opinion....

63
00:08:08,837 --> 00:08:12,299
Kane urged his country's entry into one war...

64
00:08:12,507 --> 00:08:15,135
...opposed participation in another.

65
00:08:15,385 --> 00:08:19,181
Won the election to one American president at least.

66
00:08:19,389 --> 00:08:22,017
Spoke for millions of Americans.

67
00:08:23,018 --> 00:08:25,604
Was hated by as many more.

68
00:08:25,937 --> 00:08:29,483
For 40 years appeared in Kane newsprint...

69
00:08:29,733 --> 00:08:34,071
...no public issue on which Kane papers took no stand.

70
00:08:34,488 --> 00:08:39,868
No public man whom Kane himself did not support or denounce.

71
00:08:39,910 --> 00:08:43,955
Often support, then denounce.

72
00:08:52,255 --> 00:08:55,676
Twice married, twice divorced.

73
00:08:56,009 --> 00:08:58,303
First to a president's niece...

74
00:08:58,387 --> 00:09:01,473
...Emily Norton, who left him in 1916.

75
00:09:02,057 --> 00:09:05,602
Died 1918 in a motor accident with their son.

76
00:09:08,063 --> 00:09:10,399
Sixteen years after his first marriage...

77
00:09:10,482 --> 00:09:12,818
...two weeks after his first divorce...

78
00:09:13,026 --> 00:09:15,195
...Kane married Susan Alexander...

79
00:09:15,237 --> 00:09:18,532
...singer, at the Town Hall in Trenton, New Jersey.

80
00:09:19,324 --> 00:09:24,579
For wife two, one-time opera-singing Susan Alexander...

81
00:09:24,788 --> 00:09:28,083
...Kane built Chicago's Municipal Opera House.

82
00:09:28,166 --> 00:09:30,544
Cost: $3 million.

83
00:09:30,669 --> 00:09:36,800
Conceived for Susan Alexander Kane, half-finished before she divorced him...

84
00:09:36,883 --> 00:09:38,885
...the still unfinished...

85
00:09:39,094 --> 00:09:40,470
...Xanadu.

86
00:09:40,554 --> 00:09:43,765
Cost: No man can say.

87
00:09:47,102 --> 00:09:50,397
Kane, molder of mass opinion though he was...

88
00:09:50,605 --> 00:09:53,275
...in all his life was never granted elective office...

89
00:09:53,317 --> 00:09:55,027
...by the voters of his country.

90
00:09:55,110 --> 00:09:57,863
But Kane papers were once strong indeed...

91
00:09:57,988 --> 00:10:00,490
...and once the prize seemed almost his.

92
00:10:00,782 --> 00:10:04,244
In 1916, as independent candidate for governor...

93
00:10:04,286 --> 00:10:06,747
...the best elements of the state behind him...

94
00:10:06,788 --> 00:10:11,668
...the White House seemingly the next easy step in a lightning political career...

95
00:10:11,752 --> 00:10:15,630
...then suddenly, less than one week before election...

96
00:10:16,173 --> 00:10:17,632
...defeat.

97
00:10:17,716 --> 00:10:19,926
Shameful, ignominious.

98
00:10:20,636 --> 00:10:24,139
Defeat that set back for 20 years the cause of reform in the U.S...

99
00:10:24,222 --> 00:10:27,976
...forever cancelled political chances for Charles Foster Kane.

100
00:10:31,104 --> 00:10:34,107
Then, in the first year of the Great Depression...

101
00:10:34,733 --> 00:10:36,902
...a Kane paper closes.

102
00:10:37,027 --> 00:10:40,739
For Kane, in four short years, collapse.

103
00:10:41,156 --> 00:10:46,328
Eleven Kane papers merged, more sold, scrapped.

104
00:10:56,004 --> 00:10:57,089
Is that correct?

105
00:10:57,172 --> 00:10:59,508
Don't believe everything you hear on the radio.

106
00:10:59,549 --> 00:11:03,512
-Read the Inquirer. -How were business conditions in Europe?

107
00:11:03,553 --> 00:11:07,516
How did I find business conditions in Europe, Mr. Bones?

108
00:11:07,641 --> 00:11:09,351
With great difficulty.

109
00:11:11,853 --> 00:11:13,522
Are you glad to be back?

110
00:11:13,605 --> 00:11:16,149
I'm always glad to be back. I'm an American.

111
00:11:17,025 --> 00:11:19,569
Always been an American. Anything else?

112
00:11:19,653 --> 00:11:22,739
When I was a reporter, we asked them quicker than that.

113
00:11:22,823 --> 00:11:25,325
What do you think of the chances for war in Europe?

114
00:11:25,409 --> 00:11:29,705
I talked with the responsible leaders of England, France, Germany and Italy.

115
00:11:29,871 --> 00:11:32,332
They're too intelligent to embark on a project...

116
00:11:32,374 --> 00:11:34,876
...that would mean the end of civilization.

117
00:11:35,043 --> 00:11:38,547
You can take my word for it, there will be no war.

118
00:11:42,968 --> 00:11:45,595
Kane helped to change the world...

119
00:11:45,721 --> 00:11:48,390
...but Kane's world now is history...

120
00:11:48,473 --> 00:11:51,893
...and the great yellow journalist himself lived to be history...

121
00:11:51,977 --> 00:11:54,313
...outlived his power to make it.

122
00:11:55,188 --> 00:11:59,192
Alone in his never-finished, already decaying pleasure palace...

123
00:11:59,443 --> 00:12:02,863
...aloof, seldom visited, never photographed...

124
00:12:03,155 --> 00:12:07,159
...an emperor of newsprint continued to direct his failing empire.

125
00:12:07,743 --> 00:12:10,579
Vainly attempted to sway, as he once did...

126
00:12:10,912 --> 00:12:14,333
...the destinies of a nation that had ceased to listen to him...

127
00:12:14,416 --> 00:12:16,168
...ceased to trust him.

128
00:12:18,170 --> 00:12:22,215
Then last week, as it must to all men...

129
00:12:23,175 --> 00:12:25,761
...death came to Charles Foster Kane.

130
00:12:26,720 --> 00:12:28,597
--
News on the March.

131
00:12:33,769 --> 00:12:34,978
That's it.

132
00:12:35,687 --> 00:12:38,607
Stand by. I'll tell you if we want to run it again.

133
00:12:38,690 --> 00:12:41,360
-How about it, Mr. Rawlston? -How do you like it, boys?

134
00:12:41,443 --> 00:12:45,322
-Seventy years in a man's life. -That's a lot to try to get into a newsreel.

135
00:12:45,405 --> 00:12:47,866
It's a good short, but what it needs is an angle.

136
00:12:47,949 --> 00:12:51,244
All we saw on that screen was that Charles Foster Kane is dead.

137
00:12:51,286 --> 00:12:53,413
I know that. I read the papers.

138
00:12:53,455 --> 00:12:55,957
It isn't enough to tell us what a man did...

139
00:12:56,291 --> 00:12:59,461
...you've got to tell us who he was.

140
00:12:59,503 --> 00:13:02,798
Wait a minute. What were Kane's last words?

141
00:13:02,881 --> 00:13:04,841
Do you remember, boys?

142
00:13:04,925 --> 00:13:07,386
What were the last words he said on Earth?

143
00:13:07,469 --> 00:13:09,554
Maybe he told us about himself on his deathbed.

144
00:13:09,638 --> 00:13:12,724
-Maybe he didn't. -All we saw was a big American.

145
00:13:12,808 --> 00:13:15,811
How did he differ from Ford, Hearst or John Doe?

146
00:13:16,311 --> 00:13:18,522
I tell you, a man's dying words--

147
00:13:18,647 --> 00:13:20,565
What were they? You don't read the papers.

148
00:13:20,649 --> 00:13:23,151
When Charles Foster Kane died, he said one word:

149
00:13:23,235 --> 00:13:24,236
"Rosebud."

150
00:13:24,319 --> 00:13:26,071
That's all he said? Tough guy.

151
00:13:26,154 --> 00:13:28,448
Yes, "Rosebud." Just that one word.

152
00:13:28,782 --> 00:13:30,492
-But who is she? -What was it?

153
00:13:30,534 --> 00:13:32,703
Here's a man who could've been president...

154
00:13:32,786 --> 00:13:35,080
...who was as loved, hated and talked about...

155
00:13:35,163 --> 00:13:37,708
...as any man in our time, but when he dies...

156
00:13:37,791 --> 00:13:40,043
...something is on his mind called Rosebud.

157
00:13:40,127 --> 00:13:42,587
-What does that mean? -A racehorse he bet on once.

158
00:13:42,671 --> 00:13:45,007
-That didn't come in. -But what was the race?

159
00:13:45,090 --> 00:13:46,258
Rosebud.

160
00:13:46,341 --> 00:13:47,175
-Thompson. -Yes.

161
00:13:47,217 --> 00:13:49,177
Hold this up a week, two if you must.

162
00:13:49,219 --> 00:13:51,013
Don't you think right after he's dead--

163
00:13:51,096 --> 00:13:54,224
Find out about Rosebud. Get in touch with anybody who knew him...

164
00:13:54,308 --> 00:13:55,809
...or knew him well.

165
00:13:56,143 --> 00:13:57,769
That manager of his....

166
00:13:57,853 --> 00:14:00,314
Bernstein. His second wife. She's still living.

167
00:14:00,397 --> 00:14:01,690
Susan Alexander Kane.

168
00:14:01,732 --> 00:14:04,609
-She runs a nightclub in Atlantic City. -That's right.

169
00:14:04,693 --> 00:14:08,113
See them all. Get in touch with everybody that ever worked for him...

170
00:14:08,196 --> 00:14:11,074
...whoever loved him, whoever hated his guts.

171
00:14:11,408 --> 00:14:14,411
I don't mean go through the city directory of course.

172
00:14:14,494 --> 00:14:16,079
I'll get on it right away.

173
00:14:16,163 --> 00:14:18,874
Good. Rosebud, dead or alive.

174
00:14:19,708 --> 00:14:22,294
It will probably turn out to be a very simple thing.

175
00:15:01,750 --> 00:15:03,251
Miss Alexander.

176
00:15:04,461 --> 00:15:06,713
This is Mr. Thompson, Miss Alexander.

177
00:15:06,880 --> 00:15:09,174
I want another drink, John.

178
00:15:12,094 --> 00:15:15,055
Right away. Will you have something, Mr. Thompson?

179
00:15:15,097 --> 00:15:18,642
-I'll have a highball, please. -Who told you you could sit down?

180
00:15:20,519 --> 00:15:22,437
I thought maybe we could have a talk.

181
00:15:22,521 --> 00:15:23,981
Think again.

182
00:15:25,273 --> 00:15:27,567
Can't you people leave me alone?

183
00:15:27,651 --> 00:15:29,903
I'm minding my own business, you mind yours.

184
00:15:29,945 --> 00:15:32,906
If I could just have a talk with you, Miss Alexander. I'd--

185
00:15:32,948 --> 00:15:34,491
Get out of here.

186
00:15:36,284 --> 00:15:37,536
Get out!

187
00:15:38,912 --> 00:15:40,414
-Sorry. -Get out.

188
00:15:40,706 --> 00:15:43,166
-Maybe some other time. -Get out.

189
00:15:54,177 --> 00:15:55,304
Gino.

190
00:15:57,222 --> 00:15:58,974
Get her another highball.

191
00:15:59,433 --> 00:16:01,977
She just won't talk to nobody, Mr. Thompson.

192
00:16:02,060 --> 00:16:03,145
Okay.

193
00:16:05,272 --> 00:16:07,983
-Another double? -Yeah.

194
00:16:08,942 --> 00:16:10,694
Hello, I want New York City.

195
00:16:10,777 --> 00:16:15,282
Courtland 79970. This is Atlantic City 46827. All right.

196
00:16:18,410 --> 00:16:19,953
She's....

197
00:16:20,662 --> 00:16:22,289
She'll snap out of it.

198
00:16:22,456 --> 00:16:25,751
Why, until he died, she'd just as soon talk about Mr. Kane as any--

199
00:16:25,834 --> 00:16:27,210
-Hello. -Sooner.

200
00:16:27,336 --> 00:16:29,963
This is Thompson. Let me talk to the chief.

201
00:16:31,757 --> 00:16:33,759
Mr. Rawlston? She won't talk.

202
00:16:34,009 --> 00:16:36,845
The second Mrs. Kane. About Rosebud or anything else.

203
00:16:36,928 --> 00:16:38,680
I'm calling from Atlantic City.

204
00:16:38,764 --> 00:16:42,392
Tomorrow I'll go to Philadelphia, to Thatcher Library, to see his diary.

205
00:16:42,476 --> 00:16:43,852
They're expecting me.

206
00:16:43,894 --> 00:16:47,439
Then I've a meeting with his general manager in New York. Bernstein.

207
00:16:47,522 --> 00:16:49,024
Then I'm coming back here.

208
00:16:49,066 --> 00:16:52,027
Yeah, I'll see everybody that's still alive. Goodbye.

209
00:16:54,029 --> 00:16:54,988
-Hey.... -John.

210
00:16:55,072 --> 00:16:56,907
You just might be able to help me.

211
00:16:56,990 --> 00:17:00,702
When she used to talk about Mr. Kane, did she ever mention Rosebud?

212
00:17:01,244 --> 00:17:02,579
"Rosebud"?

213
00:17:02,954 --> 00:17:04,998
Thank you, Mr. Thompson, thanks.

214
00:17:05,499 --> 00:17:09,503
As a matter of fact, just the other day, when the papers were full of it...

215
00:17:10,962 --> 00:17:13,090
...l asked her.

216
00:17:13,632 --> 00:17:15,759
She never heard of Rosebud.

217
00:17:27,938 --> 00:17:32,526
The directors of the Thatcher Memorial Library have asked me to remind you...

218
00:17:32,693 --> 00:17:35,362
...about the conditions under which you may...

219
00:17:35,404 --> 00:17:38,573
...inspect certain portions of Mr. Thatcher's unpublished memoirs.

220
00:17:38,657 --> 00:17:41,284
-l remember them. -Yes, Jennings, I'll bring him in.

221
00:17:41,368 --> 00:17:43,912
-All I want is an hour-- -Under no circumstances...

222
00:17:43,996 --> 00:17:47,749
...are direct quotes from his manuscript to be used by you. You may follow me.

223
00:17:47,833 --> 00:17:50,043
That's all right. I'm just looking for--

224
00:18:02,764 --> 00:18:04,391
Jennings.

225
00:18:09,438 --> 00:18:10,897
Thank you, Jennings.

226
00:18:11,565 --> 00:18:14,776
You will be required to leave this room at 4:30 promptly.

227
00:18:15,110 --> 00:18:17,654
You will confine yourself, it is our understanding...

228
00:18:17,738 --> 00:18:21,241
...to the chapters in Mr. Thatcher's manuscript regarding Mr. Kane.

229
00:18:21,283 --> 00:18:23,201
That's all I'm interested in.

230
00:18:24,119 --> 00:18:25,495
Thank you.

231
00:18:26,788 --> 00:18:29,750
Pages 83 to 142.

232
00:19:08,163 --> 00:19:09,956
Come on, boys.

233
00:19:10,123 --> 00:19:11,458
Be careful, Charles.

234
00:19:11,625 --> 00:19:12,459
Mrs. Kane.

235
00:19:12,501 --> 00:19:14,711
Pull your muffler around your neck, Charles.

236
00:19:14,795 --> 00:19:17,047
Mrs. Kane, I think we'll have to tell him now.

237
00:19:17,130 --> 00:19:20,634
Yes, I'll sign those papers now, Mr. Thatcher.

238
00:19:21,259 --> 00:19:24,096
You people seem to forget that I'm the boy's father.

239
00:19:24,179 --> 00:19:27,099
It's going to be done exactly the way I've told Mr. Thatcher.

240
00:19:27,182 --> 00:19:30,894
There's nothing wrong with Colorado. I don't see why we can't raise our son...

241
00:19:30,978 --> 00:19:32,771
...just 'cause we came into money.

242
00:19:32,854 --> 00:19:35,816
If I want to, I can go to court. A father has a right to.

243
00:19:35,857 --> 00:19:39,194
A boarder that beats his bill and leaves worthless stock behind....

244
00:19:39,236 --> 00:19:42,197
That property is as much my property as anybody's...

245
00:19:42,239 --> 00:19:45,200
...now that it's valuable. And if Fred Graves had any idea...

246
00:19:45,242 --> 00:19:48,829
...this would happen, he'd have made out the certificates in both our names.

247
00:19:48,912 --> 00:19:52,374
-But they're made out to Mrs. Kane. -He owed the money to both of us.

248
00:19:52,457 --> 00:19:57,295
-"The bank's decision in all matters--" -l don't hold with giving Charles to a bank--

249
00:19:57,379 --> 00:19:59,631
-Stop this nonsense. -We're a bit uneducated--

250
00:19:59,715 --> 00:20:01,842
"The bank's decision concerning his education...

251
00:20:01,883 --> 00:20:04,386
"...his places of residence, is to be final."

252
00:20:04,469 --> 00:20:08,015
-The idea of a bank being the guardian-- -Stop this nonsense, Jim.

253
00:20:08,098 --> 00:20:11,018
"We will assume full management of the Colorado Lode"...

254
00:20:11,059 --> 00:20:13,729
...which I repeat, Mrs. Kane, you are the sole owner.

255
00:20:13,812 --> 00:20:15,981
-Where do I sign, Mr. Thatcher? -Right here.

256
00:20:16,064 --> 00:20:18,483
Mary, I'm asking you for the last time.

257
00:20:19,067 --> 00:20:21,737
You'd think I hadn't been a good husband or father--

258
00:20:21,820 --> 00:20:23,363
The sum of $50,000 a year...

259
00:20:23,447 --> 00:20:26,658
...is to be paid to you and Mr. Kane as long as you both live...

260
00:20:26,742 --> 00:20:28,869
...and thereafter to the survivor.

261
00:20:29,453 --> 00:20:32,372
-Let's hope it's all for the best. -It is.

262
00:20:37,502 --> 00:20:40,839
Why I can't raise my own boy is more than I can understand.

263
00:20:40,922 --> 00:20:42,758
Go on, Mr. Thatcher.

264
00:20:42,883 --> 00:20:46,011
Everything else, the principal, as well as all monies earned...

265
00:20:46,094 --> 00:20:50,140
...is to be administered by the bank in trust for your son, Charles Foster Kane...

266
00:20:50,223 --> 00:20:52,976
...until he reaches his 25th birthday, at which time...

267
00:20:53,060 --> 00:20:55,312
...he is to come into complete possession.

268
00:20:55,395 --> 00:20:56,688
Charles!

269
00:20:58,774 --> 00:21:00,359
Go on, Mr. Thatcher.

270
00:21:01,485 --> 00:21:05,113
It's almost 5:00. Don't you think I'd better meet the boy?

271
00:21:05,947 --> 00:21:07,908
I've got his trunk all packed.

272
00:21:09,076 --> 00:21:11,244
I've had it packed for a week now.

273
00:21:13,914 --> 00:21:16,291
I've arranged for a tutor to meet us in Chicago.

274
00:21:16,333 --> 00:21:18,669
I'd have brought him here with me, but....

275
00:21:19,419 --> 00:21:21,129
-Charles. -Look, Mom.

276
00:21:21,296 --> 00:21:22,756
You'd better come inside.

277
00:21:22,798 --> 00:21:25,801
-That's quite a snowman. -l took the pipe out of his mouth.

278
00:21:25,842 --> 00:21:27,386
Did you make it yourself?.

279
00:21:27,469 --> 00:21:30,013
Maybe I'll make some teeth and whiskers.

280
00:21:30,305 --> 00:21:32,349
This is Mr. Thatcher, Charles.

281
00:21:32,766 --> 00:21:34,851
-Hello. -How do you do, Charles?

282
00:21:34,935 --> 00:21:36,478
He comes from the East.

283
00:21:36,561 --> 00:21:38,063
-Pa. -Hello, Charlie.

284
00:21:38,146 --> 00:21:39,356
Charles.

285
00:21:40,148 --> 00:21:41,483
Yes, Mommy.

286
00:21:43,068 --> 00:21:46,113
Mr. Thatcher is going to take you on a trip with him tonight.

287
00:21:46,196 --> 00:21:47,948
You'll be leaving on number 10.

288
00:21:47,990 --> 00:21:50,200
That's the train with all the lights on it.

289
00:21:50,283 --> 00:21:51,284
You going, Mom?

290
00:21:51,368 --> 00:21:54,621
No. Your mother won't be going right away, but she'll....

291
00:21:55,330 --> 00:21:57,040
Where am I going?

292
00:21:57,124 --> 00:22:00,919
You're going to see Chicago and New York and Washington, maybe. Ain't he?

293
00:22:01,003 --> 00:22:02,129
He certainly is.

294
00:22:02,212 --> 00:22:05,632
I wish I were a boy going on a trip like that for the first time.

295
00:22:05,674 --> 00:22:08,969
-Why aren't you coming with us, Mom? -We have to stay here, Charles.

296
00:22:09,011 --> 00:22:11,930
You're gonna live with Mr. Thatcher from now on, Charlie.

297
00:22:12,014 --> 00:22:15,434
You're gonna be rich. Your ma figures, well, that is...

298
00:22:15,684 --> 00:22:18,854
...me and her decided this ain't the place for you to grow up in.

299
00:22:18,895 --> 00:22:21,315
You'll probably be the richest man someday...

300
00:22:21,356 --> 00:22:23,859
-...and you ought to get-- -You won't be lonely.

301
00:22:23,942 --> 00:22:27,404
Lonely, of course not. We're going to have fine times together, we are.

302
00:22:27,487 --> 00:22:30,365
Let's shake hands. Come. I'm not that frightening, am l?

303
00:22:30,657 --> 00:22:33,035
-What do you say? Let's shake. -Why, Charles.

304
00:22:33,076 --> 00:22:34,870
-Why, you almost hurt me. -Charlie!

305
00:22:34,953 --> 00:22:37,456
Sleds aren't to hit people, but to sleigh with.

306
00:22:37,539 --> 00:22:38,498
Mom!

307
00:22:38,707 --> 00:22:40,876
-You got to go. -Jim!

308
00:22:41,585 --> 00:22:43,045
I'm sorry, Mr. Thatcher.

309
00:22:43,128 --> 00:22:44,963
What the kid needs is a good thrashing.

310
00:22:45,047 --> 00:22:46,882
-That's what you think, is it? -Yes.

311
00:22:46,965 --> 00:22:51,261
That's why he's going to be brought up where you can't get at him.

312
00:23:11,031 --> 00:23:13,325
Well, Charles...

313
00:23:15,035 --> 00:23:17,913
-...Merry Christmas. -Merry Christmas.

314
00:23:18,080 --> 00:23:20,540
And a happy New Year.

315
00:23:20,666 --> 00:23:21,917
In closing...

316
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:25,837
...may I remind you your 25th birthday, which is now approaching...

317
00:23:25,921 --> 00:23:28,632
...marks your complete independence from the firm...

318
00:23:28,715 --> 00:23:32,260
...of Thatcher & Company, as well as acquiring the full responsibility...

319
00:23:32,344 --> 00:23:34,763
...for the world's sixth largest private fortune.

320
00:23:34,846 --> 00:23:37,933
-Got that? -"The world's sixth largest private fortune."

321
00:23:38,100 --> 00:23:42,187
I don't think you realize the full importance of the position you are to occupy.

322
00:23:42,270 --> 00:23:44,481
I am therefore enclosing for your consideration...

323
00:23:44,564 --> 00:23:47,275
...a list of your holdings, extensively cross-indexed.

324
00:23:47,359 --> 00:23:49,569
-"Dear Mr. Thatcher." -It's from Mr. Kane.

325
00:23:49,611 --> 00:23:52,030
-Go on. -"Sorry, I'm not interested in gold mines...

326
00:23:52,114 --> 00:23:54,157
"...oil wells, shipping or real estate."

327
00:23:54,324 --> 00:23:56,451
Not interested?

328
00:23:56,618 --> 00:24:00,038
"One item on your list intrigues me: The New York Inquirer.

329
00:24:00,122 --> 00:24:03,000
"A little newspaper we acquired in a foreclosure proceeding.

330
00:24:03,083 --> 00:24:05,460
"Don't sell it. I am coming back to take charge.

331
00:24:05,544 --> 00:24:08,005
"l think it would be fun to run a newspaper.

332
00:24:08,088 --> 00:24:12,217
"l think it would be fun to run a newspaper."

333
00:24:20,017 --> 00:24:22,185
"Traction Trust exposed."

334
00:24:22,269 --> 00:24:24,938
"Traction Trust bleeds public white."

335
00:24:24,980 --> 00:24:28,317
"Traction Trust smashed by Inquirer."

336
00:24:28,400 --> 00:24:31,862
"Landlords refuse to clear slums."

337
00:24:32,195 --> 00:24:35,907
"Inquirer wins slum fight."

338
00:24:37,242 --> 00:24:41,163
"Wall Street backs copper swindle."

339
00:24:41,246 --> 00:24:43,373
"Copper robbers indicted."

340
00:24:43,457 --> 00:24:46,376
"Galleons of Spain off Jersey Coast."

341
00:24:46,460 --> 00:24:49,087
Is that really your idea of how to run a newspaper?

342
00:24:49,171 --> 00:24:52,549
I don't know how to run a newspaper. I try everything I can think of.

343
00:24:52,633 --> 00:24:55,594
You know there's not the slightest proof this...

344
00:24:55,677 --> 00:24:58,597
-...Armada's off the Jersey Coast. -Hello, Mr. Bernstein.

345
00:24:58,680 --> 00:25:00,140
Can you prove it isn't?

346
00:25:00,182 --> 00:25:02,934
Mr. Bernstein, I'd like you to meet Mr. Thatcher.

347
00:25:03,685 --> 00:25:04,853
-Mr. Leland. -Hello.

348
00:25:04,936 --> 00:25:06,647
Mr. Thatcher, my ex-guardian.

349
00:25:06,688 --> 00:25:11,902
We have no secrets from our readers. Thatcher is one of our devoted readers.

350
00:25:12,027 --> 00:25:15,864
He knows what's wrong with every copy of the Inquirer since I took over. Read.

351
00:25:15,906 --> 00:25:18,992
"Girls delightful in Cuba. Stop. Could send you prose poems...

352
00:25:19,034 --> 00:25:22,871
"...about scenery but don't feel right spending your money. Stop.

353
00:25:22,913 --> 00:25:25,749
"There is no war in Cuba." Signed "Wheeler." Any answer?

354
00:25:25,832 --> 00:25:30,337
Yes. Dear Wheeler: You provide the prose poems, I'll provide the war.

355
00:25:30,462 --> 00:25:33,215
-That's fine. -l like it myself. Send it right away.

356
00:25:33,256 --> 00:25:35,634
I came to see you about this campaign of yours.

357
00:25:35,717 --> 00:25:38,553
The Inquirer's campaign against the Public Transit Company.

358
00:25:38,637 --> 00:25:40,889
Do you know anything we can use against them?

359
00:25:40,931 --> 00:25:42,516
You're still the college boy, eh?

360
00:25:42,599 --> 00:25:46,311
I was expelled from college, a lot of colleges, you remember? I remember.

361
00:25:47,396 --> 00:25:50,232
I think I should remind you of a fact you have forgotten.

362
00:25:50,273 --> 00:25:54,152
You're one of the largest stockholders in the Public Transit Company.

363
00:25:54,236 --> 00:25:57,280
The trouble is, you don't realize you're talking to two people.

364
00:25:57,364 --> 00:26:00,534
As Charles Foster Kane who owns 82,364 shares...

365
00:26:00,575 --> 00:26:04,413
...of Public Transit Preferred. See, I do have a general idea of my holdings.

366
00:26:04,454 --> 00:26:06,957
I sympathize with you. Kane is a scoundrel...

367
00:26:07,249 --> 00:26:10,377
...his paper should be closed, a committee formed to boycott him.

368
00:26:10,419 --> 00:26:14,256
If you can form such a committee, put me down for a contribution of $1,000.

369
00:26:14,298 --> 00:26:17,175
On the other hand, I am the publisher of the Inquirer.

370
00:26:17,259 --> 00:26:21,346
As such it's my duty, I'll let you in on a little secret. It is also my pleasure...

371
00:26:21,513 --> 00:26:25,726
...to see that the working people of this community aren't robbed by a pack...

372
00:26:25,767 --> 00:26:28,353
...of money-mad pirates, just because...

373
00:26:28,770 --> 00:26:31,940
...they have no one to look after their interests.

374
00:26:32,357 --> 00:26:35,360
I'll let you in on another little secret, Mr. Thatcher:

375
00:26:35,444 --> 00:26:39,281
I think I'm the man to do it. You see, I have money and property.

376
00:26:39,364 --> 00:26:43,368
If I don't look after the interests of the underprivileged, somebody else will.

377
00:26:43,452 --> 00:26:45,495
Maybe somebody without money or property.

378
00:26:45,579 --> 00:26:46,913
That would be too bad.

379
00:26:46,955 --> 00:26:49,082
I saw your financial statement today.

380
00:26:49,166 --> 00:26:52,127
-Oh, did you? -Tell me, honestly...

381
00:26:52,336 --> 00:26:56,632
...don't you think it's rather unwise to continue this philanthropic enterprise...

382
00:26:56,673 --> 00:26:59,760
...this Inquirer, that is costing you $1 million a year?

383
00:26:59,801 --> 00:27:04,640
Yes. I did lose $1 million last year. I expect to lose $1 million this year.

384
00:27:04,723 --> 00:27:07,434
I expect to lose $1 million next year.

385
00:27:07,476 --> 00:27:09,728
At the rate of $1 million a year...

386
00:27:09,811 --> 00:27:13,398
...I?l have to close this place in 60 years.

387
00:27:23,158 --> 00:27:25,577
"With respect to the said newspapers...

388
00:27:25,661 --> 00:27:28,121
"...the said Charles Foster Kane...

389
00:27:28,205 --> 00:27:31,792
"...hereby relinquishes all control thereof...

390
00:27:32,209 --> 00:27:35,003
"...and of the syndicates pertaining thereto...

391
00:27:35,045 --> 00:27:41,134
"...and any and all other newspaper, press and publishing properties of any kind...

392
00:27:41,593 --> 00:27:43,971
"...and agrees to abandon all claim thereto--"

393
00:27:44,012 --> 00:27:47,391
-Which means we're bust all right. -Well, out of cash.

394
00:27:47,474 --> 00:27:49,017
All right, Mr. Bernstein.

395
00:27:49,101 --> 00:27:53,647
I've read it, Mr. Thatcher, just let me sign it and go home.

396
00:27:53,689 --> 00:27:57,067
You're too old to call me Mr. Thatcher, Charles.

397
00:27:57,192 --> 00:28:00,195
You're too old to be called anything else.

398
00:28:00,278 --> 00:28:02,489
You were always too old.

399
00:28:03,115 --> 00:28:06,827
"In consideration thereof, Thatcher & Company agrees...

400
00:28:06,868 --> 00:28:09,705
"...to pay to Charles Foster Kane, as long as he lives--"

401
00:28:09,788 --> 00:28:11,415
My allowance.

402
00:28:11,498 --> 00:28:15,377
"You will continue to maintain over your newspapers a large...

403
00:28:15,460 --> 00:28:20,215
"...measure of control. Measure of control."

404
00:28:20,716 --> 00:28:23,927
And we shall seek your advice.

405
00:28:26,513 --> 00:28:30,392
This depression is temporary.

406
00:28:31,560 --> 00:28:35,397
There's always the chance that you'll die richer than I will.

407
00:28:35,605 --> 00:28:38,066
It's a cinch I'll die richer than I was born.

408
00:28:38,150 --> 00:28:40,610
We never lost as much as we made.

409
00:28:40,694 --> 00:28:45,198
Yes, yes, but your methods. You know, Charles...

410
00:28:45,282 --> 00:28:50,537
...you never made a single investment. You always used money to....

411
00:28:51,246 --> 00:28:53,248
To buy things.

412
00:28:56,460 --> 00:28:58,754
To buy things.

413
00:28:59,713 --> 00:29:03,675
My mother should have chosen a less reliable banker.

414
00:29:10,515 --> 00:29:14,269
I always gagged on that silver spoon.

415
00:29:15,771 --> 00:29:18,273
You know, Mr. Bernstein...

416
00:29:19,941 --> 00:29:22,653
...if I hadn't been very rich...

417
00:29:23,236 --> 00:29:25,572
...l might have been a really great man.

418
00:29:25,614 --> 00:29:27,824
Don't you think you are?

419
00:29:29,826 --> 00:29:33,038
I think I did pretty well under the circumstances.

420
00:29:34,873 --> 00:29:37,292
What would you like to have been?

421
00:29:40,087 --> 00:29:42,422
Everything you hate.

422
00:29:50,931 --> 00:29:54,101
I beg your pardon, sir? What did you say?

423
00:29:54,142 --> 00:29:57,980
- 到4點30了，是不是，詹尼斯？ - 是的，夫人
-It抯 4:30. Isn't it, Jennings? -Yes, ma'am.

424
00:29:58,105 --> 00:30:00,649
You have enjoyed a very rare privilege, young man.

425
00:30:00,732 --> 00:30:03,777
-Did you find what you were looking for? -No.

426
00:30:06,238 --> 00:30:08,281
-You're not Rosebud, are you? -What?

427
00:30:08,323 --> 00:30:11,326
Rosebud, and your name is Jennings, isn't it?

428
00:30:11,493 --> 00:30:15,914
Goodbye, everybody. Thanks for the use of the hall.

429
00:30:16,665 --> 00:30:20,210
Who's a busy man, me? I'm chairman of the board.

430
00:30:20,294 --> 00:30:22,713
I got nothing but time. What do you want to know?

431
00:30:22,796 --> 00:30:27,009
We thought maybe.... If we could find out what he meant by his last words...

432
00:30:27,092 --> 00:30:29,636
-...as he was dying. -That "Rosebud"?

433
00:30:29,678 --> 00:30:32,180
Maybe some girl?

434
00:30:32,264 --> 00:30:34,516
There were a lot of them in the early days.

435
00:30:34,558 --> 00:30:37,811
It's hardly likely that Mr. Kane could have met someone casually...

436
00:30:37,853 --> 00:30:40,147
...and then 50 years later, on his deathbed--

437
00:30:40,188 --> 00:30:45,027
Well, you're pretty young, Mr. Thompson.

438
00:30:45,193 --> 00:30:48,822
A fellow will remember a lot of things you wouldn't think he'd remember.

439
00:30:48,905 --> 00:30:50,657
You take me.

440
00:30:50,699 --> 00:30:55,787
One day, back in 1896, I was crossing over to Jersey on the ferry...

441
00:30:55,996 --> 00:30:59,207
...and as we pulled out, there was another ferry pulling in...

442
00:30:59,249 --> 00:31:02,044
...and on it there was a girl waiting to get off.

443
00:31:02,878 --> 00:31:04,671
A white dress she had on.

444
00:31:04,713 --> 00:31:07,215
She was carrying a white parasol.

445
00:31:07,549 --> 00:31:10,010
I only saw her for one second.

446
00:31:10,093 --> 00:31:15,557
She didn't see me at all, but I'll bet a month hasn't gone by since...

447
00:31:16,433 --> 00:31:19,019
...that I haven't thought of that girl.

448
00:31:19,519 --> 00:31:23,732
-Who else have you been to see? -Well, I went down to Atlantic City.

449
00:31:23,774 --> 00:31:25,734
Susie?

450
00:31:25,859 --> 00:31:27,194
Thank you.

451
00:31:27,736 --> 00:31:30,614
I called her myself the day after he died.

452
00:31:30,947 --> 00:31:33,367
I thought maybe somebody ought to.

453
00:31:34,660 --> 00:31:38,955
-She couldn't even come to the phone. -I?l be seeing her again in a couple of days.

454
00:31:39,039 --> 00:31:41,041
About Rosebud, Mr. Bernstein.

455
00:31:41,249 --> 00:31:44,878
If you'd talk about anything connected with Mr. Kane that you can remember.

456
00:31:44,962 --> 00:31:46,922
You were with him from the beginning.

457
00:31:46,964 --> 00:31:52,386
From before the beginning, young fellow. And now it's after the end.

458
00:31:53,428 --> 00:31:55,764
Have you tried to see anybody except Susie?

459
00:31:55,847 --> 00:32:00,185
I haven't seen anybody else, but I've been through Walter Thatcher's journal.

460
00:32:00,268 --> 00:32:04,731
-That man was the biggest fool I ever met. -He made an awful lot of money.

461
00:32:04,815 --> 00:32:07,609
Well, it's no trick to make a lot of money...

462
00:32:08,110 --> 00:32:13,365
...if all you want is to make a lot of money.

463
00:32:15,951 --> 00:32:21,289
You take Mr. Kane. It wasn't money he wanted.

464
00:32:23,500 --> 00:32:27,713
Thatcher never did figure him out. Sometimes even I couldn't.

465
00:32:27,879 --> 00:32:31,300
You know who you ought to see? Mr. Leland.

466
00:32:31,466 --> 00:32:34,553
He was Mr. Kane's closest friend. They went to school together.

467
00:32:34,636 --> 00:32:35,804
Harvard?

468
00:32:35,887 --> 00:32:39,558
Oh, Harvard, Yale, Princeton, Cornell, Switzerland.

469
00:32:39,641 --> 00:32:42,269
He was thrown out of a lot of colleges.

470
00:32:42,477 --> 00:32:45,105
Mr. Leland never had a nickel.

471
00:32:45,439 --> 00:32:48,442
One of those families where the father is worth $10 million...

472
00:32:48,525 --> 00:32:52,821
...then one day he shoots himself, and it turns out there's nothing but debts.

473
00:32:54,489 --> 00:32:56,742
He was with Mr. Kane and me...

474
00:32:57,743 --> 00:33:00,662
...the first day Mr. Kane took over the Inquirer.

475
00:33:08,337 --> 00:33:10,839
Take a good look at it, Jedediah.

476
00:33:11,089 --> 00:33:14,968
It's going to look a lot different one of these days. Come on.

477
00:33:23,727 --> 00:33:27,022
There ain't no bedrooms in this joint. That's a newspaper building.

478
00:33:27,105 --> 00:33:31,902
You're getting paid, mister, for opinions or for hauling?

479
00:33:34,696 --> 00:33:37,616
-Jedediah. -After you, Mr. Kane.

480
00:33:49,836 --> 00:33:52,047
Excuse me, sir, but l....

481
00:33:55,008 --> 00:33:57,386
Welcome, Mr. Kane.

482
00:33:59,471 --> 00:34:02,224
Welcome to the Inquirer, Mr. Kane.

483
00:34:02,265 --> 00:34:04,559
I am Herbert Carter, the editor-in-chief.

484
00:34:04,643 --> 00:34:08,563
-Thank you, Mr. Carter. This is Mr. Leland-- -How do you do, Mr. Leland?

485
00:34:08,605 --> 00:34:11,566
Our new dramatic critic. I hope I haven't made a mistake.

486
00:34:11,608 --> 00:34:13,860
-It is dramatic critic, right? -That's right.

487
00:34:13,902 --> 00:34:17,531
-Are they standing for me? -You? Oh, Mr. Kane.

488
00:34:18,031 --> 00:34:18,907
Standing?

489
00:34:18,949 --> 00:34:20,575
-How do you do? -How do you do?

490
00:34:20,659 --> 00:34:23,036
I thought it would be a nice little gesture.

491
00:34:23,078 --> 00:34:26,206
-Ask them to sit down, will you, please. -The new publisher.

492
00:34:26,790 --> 00:34:30,669
-You may resume your duties, gentlemen. -Thank you.

493
00:34:30,752 --> 00:34:34,089
-l didn't know your plans. -l don't know my plans myself.

494
00:34:34,423 --> 00:34:36,758
-Matter of fact, I haven't got any plans. -No?

495
00:34:36,800 --> 00:34:39,845
Except to get out a newspaper.

496
00:34:44,099 --> 00:34:46,435
-Mr. Bernstein. -Yes, Mr. Kane.

497
00:34:46,518 --> 00:34:48,895
Mr. Carter, this is Mr. Bernstein.

498
00:34:48,979 --> 00:34:53,984
-Mr. Bernstein is my general manager. -How do you do, Mr. Carter?

499
00:34:54,067 --> 00:34:55,944
-Mr. Carter. -How do you do?

500
00:34:56,945 --> 00:34:59,281
-Yes, Mr. Bernstein. -Stein.

501
00:34:59,364 --> 00:35:03,035
-Kane. -Mr. Carter, is this your office?

502
00:35:03,118 --> 00:35:07,247
My little private sanctum is at your disposal.

503
00:35:07,331 --> 00:35:10,751
-Excuse me. -But I don't think I understand.

504
00:35:11,126 --> 00:35:15,130
Mr. Carter, I'm going to live right here in your office as long as I have to.

505
00:35:16,256 --> 00:35:18,216
Live here? Yes?

506
00:35:18,300 --> 00:35:21,261
-Excuse me. -But a morning newspaper, after all--

507
00:35:21,345 --> 00:35:22,179
Excuse me.

508
00:35:22,262 --> 00:35:26,141
We're practically closed for 12 hours a day.

509
00:35:26,433 --> 00:35:29,561
That's one of the things that's going to have to be changed here.

510
00:35:29,645 --> 00:35:33,190
-The news goes on for 24 hours a day. -24?

511
00:35:33,315 --> 00:35:36,652
-That's right. -Excuse me.

512
00:35:36,735 --> 00:35:38,445
It's impossible....

513
00:35:38,487 --> 00:35:43,533
I've drawn that cartoon. I'm no good as a cartoonist.

514
00:35:43,617 --> 00:35:45,202
You certainly aren't.

515
00:35:45,285 --> 00:35:47,120
You're the dramatic critic, Leland.

516
00:35:47,162 --> 00:35:49,164
-You still eating? -I? still hungry.

517
00:35:49,206 --> 00:35:51,708
Here's a front-page story in the Chronicle...

518
00:35:51,792 --> 00:35:54,920
...about a Mrs. Harry Silverstone in Brooklyn who's missing.

519
00:35:55,003 --> 00:35:59,633
She's probably murdered. Why isn't there something about it in the Inquirer?

520
00:35:59,675 --> 00:36:01,510
We're running a newspaper...

521
00:36:01,551 --> 00:36:04,513
-I? absolutely starving to death. -...not a scandal sheet.

522
00:36:05,389 --> 00:36:06,640
That's all right.

523
00:36:06,682 --> 00:36:10,686
Mr. Carter, here is a three-column headline in the Chronicle.

524
00:36:10,769 --> 00:36:13,313
Why hasn't the Inquirer a three-column headline?

525
00:36:13,397 --> 00:36:14,856
The news wasn't big enough.

526
00:36:14,940 --> 00:36:19,695
Mr. Carter, if the headline is big enough, it makes the news big enough.

527
00:36:19,736 --> 00:36:22,364
-That's right! -The murder of Mrs. Harry Silverstone--

528
00:36:22,447 --> 00:36:24,658
There's no proof that she was murdered, or dead.

529
00:36:24,700 --> 00:36:27,577
It says she's missing. The neighbors are getting suspicious.

530
00:36:27,661 --> 00:36:30,539
It's not our function to report the gossip of housewives.

531
00:36:30,622 --> 00:36:34,876
If we were interested in that kind of thing, we could fill the paper twice over, daily.

532
00:36:34,918 --> 00:36:38,297
That's the kind of thing we are going to be interested in from now on.

533
00:36:38,380 --> 00:36:41,341
I want you to send your best man to see Mr. Silverstone.

534
00:36:41,425 --> 00:36:45,304
Have him tell Mr. Silverstone if he doesn't produce his wife, Mrs. Silverstone...

535
00:36:45,387 --> 00:36:47,472
...the Inquirer will have him arrested.

536
00:36:47,556 --> 00:36:50,017
Tell Mr. Silverstone he's a detective from....

537
00:36:50,100 --> 00:36:52,561
-Central Office. -The Central Office.

538
00:36:52,894 --> 00:36:57,399
If Mr. Silverstone gets suspicious and asks to see your man's badge...

539
00:36:57,482 --> 00:37:01,320
...your man is to get indignant and call Mr. Silverstone an anarchist.

540
00:37:01,570 --> 00:37:04,781
Loudly, so the neighbors can hear. You ready for dinner, Jedediah?

541
00:37:04,865 --> 00:37:08,118
I can't see that the function of a respectable newspaper--

542
00:37:08,243 --> 00:37:11,371
Thank you so much, Mr. Carter. Goodbye.

543
00:37:11,955 --> 00:37:13,206
Goodbye.

544
00:37:13,915 --> 00:37:16,752
Paper! Read all about it!

545
00:37:17,002 --> 00:37:19,921
Read all about it in the early morning Chronicle.

546
00:37:20,005 --> 00:37:22,466
The mystery of the lady that vanished in Brooklyn.

547
00:37:23,884 --> 00:37:26,386
Read all about it in the early morning Chronicle.

548
00:37:40,067 --> 00:37:43,695
We'll be on the street soon, Charlie, another 10 minutes.

549
00:37:43,820 --> 00:37:46,531
Three hours and 50 minutes late, but we did it.

550
00:37:46,615 --> 00:37:48,158
-Tired? -A tough day.

551
00:37:48,241 --> 00:37:49,868
-A wasted day. -Wasted?

552
00:37:49,951 --> 00:37:52,871
You only made the paper over four times tonight, that's all.

553
00:37:53,121 --> 00:37:56,583
I've changed the front page a little, Mr. Bernstein. That's not enough.

554
00:37:56,625 --> 00:38:00,879
There's something I've got to get into this paper besides pictures and print.

555
00:38:01,463 --> 00:38:04,591
I've got to make the New York Inquirer as important to New York...

556
00:38:04,633 --> 00:38:06,551
...as the gas in that light.

557
00:38:07,177 --> 00:38:08,971
What are you going to do, Charlie?

558
00:38:09,054 --> 00:38:13,225
My Declaration of Principles. Don't smile, Jedediah.

559
00:38:13,642 --> 00:38:16,103
I've got it all written out here.

560
00:38:16,645 --> 00:38:19,690
You don't want to make any promises you don't want to keep.

561
00:38:19,773 --> 00:38:21,441
These'll be kept.

562
00:38:22,317 --> 00:38:24,820
"I?l provide the people of this city...

563
00:38:25,153 --> 00:38:28,740
"...with a daily paper that will tell all the news honestly.

564
00:38:29,992 --> 00:38:33,328
-"l will also provide--" -That's two sentences starting with "l."

565
00:38:33,412 --> 00:38:35,998
People will know who's responsible...

566
00:38:36,081 --> 00:38:40,127
...and they'll get the truth in the Inquirer, quickly, simply and entertainingly.

567
00:38:40,210 --> 00:38:43,588
No special interests will be allowed to interfere with that truth.

568
00:38:43,672 --> 00:38:47,551
"l will also provide them with a fighting and tireless champion of their rights...

569
00:38:47,634 --> 00:38:50,262
"...as citizens and as human beings."

570
00:38:50,929 --> 00:38:52,222
Signed:

571
00:38:52,597 --> 00:38:55,183
"Charles Foster Kane."

572
00:38:57,144 --> 00:38:59,813
-Can I have that, Charlie? -I? going to print it.

573
00:38:59,896 --> 00:39:01,356
Solly!

574
00:39:02,357 --> 00:39:03,442
Yes, Mr. Kane?

575
00:39:03,525 --> 00:39:06,361
I want you to run this editorial in a box on the front page.

576
00:39:06,445 --> 00:39:07,946
This morning's front page?

577
00:39:08,030 --> 00:39:11,074
That's right, Solly, that means we'll have to remake again.

578
00:39:11,158 --> 00:39:12,993
-Yes. -Go down and tell them.

579
00:39:13,035 --> 00:39:14,369
All right.

580
00:39:14,661 --> 00:39:18,332
When you're through with that, I'd like to have it back.

581
00:39:20,042 --> 00:39:23,378
I'd like to keep that particular piece of paper myself.

582
00:39:23,420 --> 00:39:27,507
I have a hunch it might turn out to be something pretty important.

583
00:39:27,758 --> 00:39:29,968
-A document... -Sure.

584
00:39:30,052 --> 00:39:33,472
...like the Declaration of Independence and the Constitution...

585
00:39:34,222 --> 00:39:36,808
...and my first report card at school.

586
00:40:05,087 --> 00:40:08,423
I know you're tired, gentlemen, but I brought you here for a reason.

587
00:40:08,465 --> 00:40:12,135
-This little pilgrimage will do us good. -The Chronicle's a good newspaper.

588
00:40:12,219 --> 00:40:15,305
Chronicle's a good idea for a newspaper. Notice the circulation.

589
00:40:15,389 --> 00:40:20,268
-495,000. But look who's working for the Chronicle.

590
00:40:20,352 --> 00:40:23,563
-With them, it's no trick to get circulation. -You're right.

591
00:40:23,772 --> 00:40:27,067
You know how long it took the Chronicle to get that staff together?

592
00:40:27,109 --> 00:40:29,903
-Twenty years. -Twenty years?

593
00:40:30,445 --> 00:40:35,534
Six years ago, I looked at a picture of the world's greatest newspaper men.

594
00:40:35,617 --> 00:40:37,828
I felt like a kid in front of a candy store.

595
00:40:37,911 --> 00:40:41,915
Tonight, six years later, I got my candy, all of it.

596
00:40:42,457 --> 00:40:44,418
Welcome, gentlemen, to the Inquirer.

597
00:40:44,459 --> 00:40:47,587
Make an extra copy of that picture and mail it to the Chronicle.

598
00:40:49,214 --> 00:40:52,592
It'll make you all happy to learn that our circulation this morning...

599
00:40:52,634 --> 00:40:55,470
...was the greatest in New York: 684,000.

600
00:40:56,972 --> 00:40:59,975
-684,132.

601
00:41:00,225 --> 00:41:01,393
Right.

602
00:41:01,935 --> 00:41:05,605
I hope you'll forgive my rudeness in taking leave of you.

603
00:41:05,689 --> 00:41:08,150
I'm going abroad next week for a vacation.

604
00:41:08,233 --> 00:41:11,778
I've promised my doctor for sometime that I would leave when I could.

605
00:41:11,862 --> 00:41:13,822
I now realize I can't.

606
00:41:14,364 --> 00:41:16,992
Say, Mr. Kane, as long as you're promising...

607
00:41:17,075 --> 00:41:20,996
...there's a lot of pictures and statues in Europe you ain't bought yet.

608
00:41:21,163 --> 00:41:23,081
You can't blame me, Mr. Bernstein.

609
00:41:23,498 --> 00:41:26,168
They've been making statues for 2,000 years.

610
00:41:26,251 --> 00:41:28,337
And I've only been buying for five.

611
00:41:28,420 --> 00:41:30,964
-Promise me, Mr. Kane. -l promise, Mr. Bernstein.

612
00:41:31,048 --> 00:41:33,884
-Thank you. -Mr. Bernstein?

613
00:41:33,967 --> 00:41:37,220
You don't expect me to keep any of those promises, do you?

614
00:41:39,014 --> 00:41:41,141
And now, gentlemen!

615
00:41:41,183 --> 00:41:44,436
Your complete attention, if you please.

616
00:41:50,359 --> 00:41:53,445
Are we going to declare war on Spain?

617
00:42:01,286 --> 00:42:04,831
Oh, mama, here they come. Shoot me while I'm happy.

618
00:42:13,006 --> 00:42:18,303
I said, "Are we going to declare war on Spain, or are we not?"

619
00:42:18,387 --> 00:42:20,222
The Inquirer already has.

620
00:42:20,555 --> 00:42:24,393
You long-faced, overdressed anarchist.

621
00:42:24,601 --> 00:42:26,395
I'm not overdressed.

622
00:42:26,520 --> 00:42:29,690
You are, too. Mr. Bernstein, look at his necktie.

623
00:42:29,773 --> 00:42:31,942
Let's have the song about Charlie.

624
00:42:32,025 --> 00:42:35,404
-Is there a song about Charlie? -Is there a song about you, Mr. Kane?

625
00:42:35,487 --> 00:42:39,825
You buy a bag of peanuts in this town, you get a song written about you.

626
00:42:49,668 --> 00:42:52,004
I've seen that fellow. He's good.

627
00:42:52,212 --> 00:42:53,588
Good evening, Mr. Kane.

628
00:42:53,672 --> 00:42:56,633
"There is a man, a certain man

629
00:42:56,842 --> 00:43:00,178
"And for the poor you may be sure that he'll do all he can

630
00:43:00,304 --> 00:43:03,765
"Who is this one? This favorite son

631
00:43:03,807 --> 00:43:06,768
"Just by his action has the traction magnates on the run

632
00:43:06,852 --> 00:43:10,147
"Who loves to smoke, enjoys a joke

633
00:43:10,355 --> 00:43:13,400
"Who wouldn't get a bit upset if he were really broke

634
00:43:13,567 --> 00:43:16,737
"With wealth and fame he's still the same

635
00:43:16,778 --> 00:43:20,324
"I?l bet you $5 you're not alive if you don't know his name

636
00:43:26,121 --> 00:43:27,581
"What is his name?

637
00:43:27,623 --> 00:43:30,167
就是查利·凱恩，凱恩先生
"It抯 Charlie Kane, it's Mister Kane!

638
00:43:30,250 --> 00:43:34,087
"He doesn't like that Mister He likes good old Charlie Kane"

639
00:43:49,728 --> 00:43:52,773
- 真熱鬧啊，是嗎？多熱鬧的宴會！ - 是啊
-Isn抰 it wonderful? Such a party. -Yes.

640
00:43:52,814 --> 00:43:54,608
What's the matter?

641
00:43:54,775 --> 00:43:58,070
"Who says a miss was made to kiss

642
00:43:58,153 --> 00:44:01,406
"And when he meets one always tries to do exactly this

643
00:44:01,490 --> 00:44:04,534
"Who buys the food, who buys the drinks

644
00:44:04,618 --> 00:44:07,996
"Who thinks that dough was made to spend and acts the way he thinks

645
00:44:08,038 --> 00:44:10,791
"Now is it, Joe, no, no, no"

646
00:44:11,500 --> 00:44:14,169
Bernstein, these men who are now with the Inquirer...

647
00:44:14,252 --> 00:44:16,964
...who were with the Chronicle until yesterday--

648
00:44:17,089 --> 00:44:18,674
Oh, mama, please.

649
00:44:18,799 --> 00:44:20,300
-Give me that. -What? The blonde?

650
00:44:20,342 --> 00:44:24,012
-No, the brunette. -Where did you learn that, Charlie?

651
00:44:25,305 --> 00:44:28,475
Bernstein, these men who were with the Chronicle...

652
00:44:28,558 --> 00:44:31,645
...weren't they just as devoted to the Chronicle policy...

653
00:44:31,770 --> 00:44:33,981
...as they are now to our policies?

654
00:44:34,064 --> 00:44:36,817
Sure, they're just like anybody else.

655
00:44:37,025 --> 00:44:39,111
They got work to do, they do it.

656
00:44:39,403 --> 00:44:42,364
Only they happen to be the best men in the business.

657
00:44:50,706 --> 00:44:55,043
Do we stand for the same things the Chronicle stands for?

658
00:44:55,210 --> 00:44:57,004
Certainly not.

659
00:44:58,005 --> 00:45:03,051
Listen, Mr. Kane, he'll have them changed to his kind of newspapermen in a week.

660
00:45:03,218 --> 00:45:07,055
There's always a chance, of course, that they'll change Mr. Kane.

661
00:45:07,723 --> 00:45:09,141
Without his knowing it.

662
00:45:12,602 --> 00:45:16,189
Mr. Leland, I got a cable from Mr. Kane!

663
00:45:18,608 --> 00:45:21,320
-l got a cable here from Mr. Kane. -What?

664
00:45:21,403 --> 00:45:23,322
-From Paris, France. -What?

665
00:45:23,405 --> 00:45:26,408
-From Paris, France. -Come on in.

666
00:45:27,326 --> 00:45:32,247
"Who by his action has the traction magnates on the run"

667
00:45:32,581 --> 00:45:37,711
It's a good thing he promised not to send back any more statues.

668
00:45:39,046 --> 00:45:41,757
Look, he wants to buy the world's biggest diamond.

669
00:45:41,840 --> 00:45:45,385
Why didn't you go to Europe with him? He wanted you to.

670
00:45:45,469 --> 00:45:48,221
I wanted Charlie to have fun, with me along....

671
00:45:48,388 --> 00:45:50,557
Bernstein, am I a stuffed shirt?

672
00:45:50,641 --> 00:45:55,354
Am I a horse-faced hypocrite? Am I a New England schoolmarm?

673
00:45:56,104 --> 00:45:57,439
Yes.

674
00:45:58,690 --> 00:46:03,236
If you thought I'd answer you different from what Mr. Kane tells you, I wouldn't.

675
00:46:03,320 --> 00:46:04,780
"World's biggest diamond."

676
00:46:04,821 --> 00:46:07,199
I didn't know Charlie was collecting diamonds.

677
00:46:07,282 --> 00:46:08,784
He ain't.

678
00:46:08,867 --> 00:46:12,079
He's collecting somebody that's collecting diamonds.

679
00:46:12,537 --> 00:46:15,624
Anyway, he ain't only collecting statues.

680
00:46:17,709 --> 00:46:19,795
"Welcome home, Mr. Kane...

681
00:46:19,878 --> 00:46:23,632
"...from 467 employees of the New York Inquirer."

682
00:46:23,715 --> 00:46:25,258
Here he comes!

683
00:46:29,304 --> 00:46:31,098
Welcome, Mr. Kane.

684
00:46:31,890 --> 00:46:33,725
-l know I've a moustache. -It looks awful.

685
00:46:33,809 --> 00:46:37,396
Have we got a society editor?

686
00:46:37,479 --> 00:46:40,399
-Right here, Mr. Kane. -Miss Townsend is the society editor.

687
00:46:40,482 --> 00:46:43,151
Miss Townsend, this is Mr. Charles Foster Kane.

688
00:46:44,319 --> 00:46:48,448
Miss Townsend, I've been away so long. I don't know your routine.

689
00:46:49,616 --> 00:46:52,744
I got a little social announcement.

690
00:46:52,828 --> 00:46:56,790
I wish you wouldn't treat this any differently than you would any other...

691
00:46:56,999 --> 00:46:58,625
...social announcement.

692
00:46:59,293 --> 00:47:02,337
Mr. Kane, on behalf of all the employees of the Inquirer--

693
00:47:02,421 --> 00:47:05,966
Mr. Bernstein, thank you very much, everybody, l....

694
00:47:06,842 --> 00:47:10,971
I'm sorry, I can't accept it now.

695
00:47:11,138 --> 00:47:12,597
Goodbye.

696
00:47:13,265 --> 00:47:15,475
Say, he was in an awful hurry.

697
00:47:16,435 --> 00:47:18,562
Hey, everybody, look out here.

698
00:47:18,687 --> 00:47:20,647
Let's go to the window.

699
00:47:20,856 --> 00:47:23,775
-Mr. Leland! Mr. Bernstein! -Yes, Ms. Townsend?

700
00:47:23,859 --> 00:47:26,695
This announcement: "Mr. and Mrs. Thomas Monroe Norton...

701
00:47:26,778 --> 00:47:30,324
"...announce the engagement of their daughter, Emily Monroe Norton...

702
00:47:30,407 --> 00:47:32,159
"...to Mr. Charles Foster Kane."

703
00:47:32,242 --> 00:47:33,785
Come on.

704
00:47:36,288 --> 00:47:41,168
Emily Monroe Norton, she's the niece of the President of the United States.

705
00:47:41,376 --> 00:47:43,253
President's niece?

706
00:47:43,545 --> 00:47:46,882
Before he's through, she'll be a president's wife.

707
00:47:55,223 --> 00:47:58,644
The way things turned out, I don't need to tell you.

708
00:47:58,727 --> 00:48:01,438
Miss Emily Norton was no rosebud.

709
00:48:01,563 --> 00:48:03,649
It didn't end very well, did it?

710
00:48:04,066 --> 00:48:05,359
It ended.

711
00:48:06,443 --> 00:48:09,946
Then there was Susie. That ended, too.

712
00:48:12,240 --> 00:48:14,868
You know, Mr. Thompson, I was thinking...

713
00:48:15,702 --> 00:48:18,622
...this Rosebud you're trying to find out about....

714
00:48:19,873 --> 00:48:21,208
Yes?

715
00:48:25,587 --> 00:48:28,006
Maybe that was something he lost.

716
00:48:28,840 --> 00:48:32,219
Mr. Kane was a man who lost almost everything he had.

717
00:48:32,886 --> 00:48:35,138
You ought to see Jed Leland.

718
00:48:35,222 --> 00:48:38,392
Of course, he and Mr. Kane didn't exactly see eye to eye.

719
00:48:38,475 --> 00:48:40,477
You take the Spanish-American war.

720
00:48:40,560 --> 00:48:43,939
I guess Mr. Leland was right. That was Mr. Kane's war.

721
00:48:43,981 --> 00:48:46,984
We didn't really have anything to fight about.

722
00:48:47,776 --> 00:48:51,363
Do you think if it hadn't been for that war of Mr. Kane's...

723
00:48:51,655 --> 00:48:54,408
...we'd have the Panama Canal?

724
00:48:55,075 --> 00:48:57,577
I wish I knew where Mr. Leland was.

725
00:48:58,161 --> 00:49:01,331
A lot of the time now they don't tell me these things.

726
00:49:02,624 --> 00:49:04,459
Maybe even he's dead.

727
00:49:04,584 --> 00:49:06,295
In case you'd like to know...

728
00:49:06,378 --> 00:49:09,339
...he's at the Huntington Memorial Hospital on 180th Street.

729
00:49:09,423 --> 00:49:10,924
You don't say. I had--

730
00:49:10,966 --> 00:49:13,885
Nothing particular the matter with him, they tell me, just....

731
00:49:13,969 --> 00:49:16,555
Just old age.

732
00:49:17,848 --> 00:49:22,769
It's the only disease that you don't look forward to being cured of.

733
00:49:34,865 --> 00:49:37,743
I can remember absolutely everything, young man.

734
00:49:37,826 --> 00:49:39,119
That's my curse.

735
00:49:39,161 --> 00:49:43,749
That's one of the greatest curses ever inflicted on the human race: memory.

736
00:49:44,708 --> 00:49:49,546
I was his oldest friend, and as far as I was concerned, he behaved like a swine.

737
00:49:49,796 --> 00:49:53,884
Not that Charlie was ever brutal. He just did brutal things.

738
00:49:54,676 --> 00:49:58,930
Maybe I wasn't his friend, but if I wasn't, he never had one.

739
00:49:59,181 --> 00:50:02,809
Maybe I was what you nowadays call a stooge.

740
00:50:02,851 --> 00:50:05,228
You were about to say something about Rosebud.

741
00:50:05,312 --> 00:50:07,522
Do you happen to have a good cigar?

742
00:50:07,606 --> 00:50:11,485
I've got a young physician here who thinks I'm going to give up smoking.

743
00:50:11,526 --> 00:50:13,862
No, I'm afraid I haven't. Sorry.

744
00:50:14,196 --> 00:50:15,864
I changed the subject, didn't l?

745
00:50:15,947 --> 00:50:18,408
What a disagreeable old man I have become.

746
00:50:19,368 --> 00:50:24,206
You're a reporter and you want to know what I think about Charlie Kane.

747
00:50:27,626 --> 00:50:30,170
I suppose he had some private sort of greatness.

748
00:50:30,212 --> 00:50:31,964
But he kept it to himself.

749
00:50:32,047 --> 00:50:35,008
He never gave himself away. He never gave anything away.

750
00:50:35,050 --> 00:50:38,053
He just left you a tip.

751
00:50:39,554 --> 00:50:40,931
He had a generous mind.

752
00:50:41,014 --> 00:50:44,184
I don't suppose anybody ever had so many opinions.

753
00:50:44,476 --> 00:50:47,187
But he never believed in anything except Charlie Kane.

754
00:50:47,270 --> 00:50:50,899
He never had a conviction except Charlie Kane in his life.

755
00:50:51,191 --> 00:50:53,402
I suppose he died without one.

756
00:50:53,568 --> 00:50:56,154
That must have been pretty unpleasant.

757
00:50:56,488 --> 00:51:01,159
Of course, a lot of us check out without having any special convictions about death.

758
00:51:01,243 --> 00:51:04,496
But we do know what we're leaving. We do believe in something.

759
00:51:05,163 --> 00:51:08,208
Are you absolutely sure you haven't got a cigar?

760
00:51:08,375 --> 00:51:10,377
-Sorry, Mr. Leland. -Never mind.

761
00:51:10,419 --> 00:51:14,047
-What do you know about Rosebud? -"Rosebud"?

762
00:51:15,048 --> 00:51:18,260
His dying words: "Rosebud."

763
00:51:18,593 --> 00:51:20,721
I saw that in the Inquirer.

764
00:51:21,722 --> 00:51:24,725
I never believed anything I saw in the Inquirer.

765
00:51:24,766 --> 00:51:26,226
Anything else?

766
00:51:26,268 --> 00:51:29,980
I can tell you about Emily. I went to dancing school with Emily.

767
00:51:30,397 --> 00:51:32,149
I was very graceful.

768
00:51:33,108 --> 00:51:36,486
-We were talking about the first Mrs. Kane. -What was she like?

769
00:51:36,570 --> 00:51:39,072
She was like all the girls I knew in dancing school.

770
00:51:39,156 --> 00:51:42,200
Very nice girl. Emily was a little nicer.

771
00:51:43,660 --> 00:51:45,746
After the first couple of months...

772
00:51:45,787 --> 00:51:50,125
...she and Charlie didn't see much of each other except at breakfast.

773
00:51:50,792 --> 00:51:53,420
It was a marriage just like any other marriage.

774
00:52:03,639 --> 00:52:05,349
-You're beautiful. -l can't be.

775
00:52:05,432 --> 00:52:07,184
Yes, you are. You're very beautiful.

776
00:52:07,267 --> 00:52:09,811
I've never been to six parties in one night before.

777
00:52:10,062 --> 00:52:12,814
- 我也從來沒有這樣晚才起床 - 這只是個習慣問題
-I've never been up this late. -It抯 a matter of habit.

778
00:52:12,898 --> 00:52:15,859
-What will the servants think? -That we enjoyed ourselves.

779
00:52:15,942 --> 00:52:18,445
Why do you have to go straight off to the newspaper?

780
00:52:18,528 --> 00:52:22,240
You never should've married a newspaperman, they're worse than sailors.

781
00:52:23,158 --> 00:52:25,077
I absolutely adore you.

782
00:52:25,911 --> 00:52:28,830
Charles, even newspapermen have to sleep.

783
00:52:30,332 --> 00:52:33,752
I'll call Mr. Bernstein and have him put off my appointments till noon.

784
00:52:35,087 --> 00:52:36,171
What time is it?

785
00:52:36,838 --> 00:52:38,465
I don't know. It's late.

786
00:52:40,842 --> 00:52:42,803
It's early.

787
00:52:42,886 --> 00:52:44,596
Charles....

788
00:52:45,180 --> 00:52:48,183
Do you know how long you kept me waiting last night...

789
00:52:48,225 --> 00:52:51,061
...when you went to the newspaper for 10 minutes?

790
00:52:51,311 --> 00:52:54,481
What do you do in a newspaper in the middle of the night?

791
00:52:54,648 --> 00:52:59,653
My dear, your only correspondent is the Inquirer.

792
00:52:59,695 --> 00:53:02,531
Sometimes I think I'd prefer a rival of flesh and blood.

793
00:53:02,572 --> 00:53:05,158
I don't spend that much time on the newspaper.

794
00:53:05,200 --> 00:53:09,329
It isn't just the time. It's what you print, attacking the President.

795
00:53:09,371 --> 00:53:12,624
-You mean Uncle John. -l mean the President of the United States.

796
00:53:12,708 --> 00:53:15,168
He's still Uncle John and a well-meaning fathead...

797
00:53:15,210 --> 00:53:18,630
...who's letting a pack of high-pressure crooks run his administration.

798
00:53:18,714 --> 00:53:22,509
-This whole oil scandal-- -He happens to be the President, not you.

799
00:53:22,551 --> 00:53:25,387
That's a mistake that will be corrected one of these days.

800
00:53:25,470 --> 00:53:30,726
Your Mr. Bernstein sent Junior the most incredible atrocity yesterday.

801
00:53:31,727 --> 00:53:34,396
I simply can't have it in the nursery.

802
00:53:36,523 --> 00:53:39,818
Mr. Bernstein is apt to pay a visit to the nursery now and then.

803
00:53:39,901 --> 00:53:41,486
Does he have to?

804
00:53:42,529 --> 00:53:43,572
Yes.

805
00:53:43,697 --> 00:53:48,118
-Really, Charles, people will think-- -What I tell them to think.

806
00:54:06,928 --> 00:54:08,972
Wasn't he ever in love with her?

807
00:54:09,139 --> 00:54:10,891
He married for love.

808
00:54:11,808 --> 00:54:13,185
Love.

809
00:54:13,852 --> 00:54:15,562
That is why he did everything.

810
00:54:15,646 --> 00:54:18,607
That's why he went into politics. It seems we weren't enough.

811
00:54:18,690 --> 00:54:21,735
He wanted all the voters to love him, too.

812
00:54:22,444 --> 00:54:24,946
All he wanted out of life was love.

813
00:54:25,781 --> 00:54:28,992
That's Charlie's story. How he lost it.

814
00:54:29,201 --> 00:54:32,412
You see, he just didn't have any to give.

815
00:54:33,413 --> 00:54:36,208
He loved Charlie Kane, of course.

816
00:54:36,792 --> 00:54:38,210
Very dearly.

817
00:54:38,961 --> 00:54:41,171
And his mother, I guess he always loved her.

818
00:54:41,254 --> 00:54:44,299
-How about his second wife? -Susan Alexander?

819
00:54:45,092 --> 00:54:47,260
You know what Charlie called her?

820
00:54:47,469 --> 00:54:50,222
The day after he'd met her, he told me about her.

821
00:54:50,389 --> 00:54:55,310
He said she was "a cross-section of the American public."

822
00:54:57,521 --> 00:55:01,149
I guess he couldn't help it. She must have had something for him.

823
00:55:01,316 --> 00:55:04,069
That first night, according to Charlie...

824
00:55:05,153 --> 00:55:07,489
...all she had was a toothache.

825
00:55:38,520 --> 00:55:41,064
What are you laughing at, young lady?

826
00:55:42,941 --> 00:55:45,694
-What's the matter with you? -Toothache.

827
00:55:45,777 --> 00:55:47,529
-What? -Toothache.

828
00:55:47,612 --> 00:55:48,947
Toothache?

829
00:55:52,367 --> 00:55:54,411
You mean you've got a toothache.

830
00:55:55,329 --> 00:55:58,206
-What's funny about that? -You're funny, mister.

831
00:55:58,290 --> 00:56:00,876
-You've got dirt on your face. -Not dirt, it's mud.

832
00:56:00,959 --> 00:56:03,253
Do you want some hot water? I live right here.

833
00:56:03,337 --> 00:56:05,297
What's that, young lady?

834
00:56:06,548 --> 00:56:09,009
I said, if you wanted some hot water...

835
00:56:09,301 --> 00:56:13,472
...l could get you some...hot water.

836
00:56:17,059 --> 00:56:19,394
All right, thank you very much.

837
00:56:33,742 --> 00:56:35,702
Do I look any better now?

838
00:56:35,744 --> 00:56:40,499
-This medicine doesn't do a bit of good. -What you need is to get your mind off it.

839
00:56:45,671 --> 00:56:48,799
Excuse me, but my landlady prefers me to keep this door open...

840
00:56:49,049 --> 00:56:52,052
-...when I have a gentleman caller. -All right.

841
00:56:52,094 --> 00:56:54,221
You have got a toothache, haven't you?

842
00:56:54,262 --> 00:56:57,349
-l surely have. -Why don't you try laughing at me again?

843
00:56:57,432 --> 00:56:59,393
-What? -I? still pretty funny.

844
00:56:59,434 --> 00:57:02,062
I know, but you don't want me to laugh at you.

845
00:57:02,104 --> 00:57:04,189
I don't want your tooth to hurt, either.

846
00:57:04,272 --> 00:57:05,565
Look at me.

847
00:57:05,983 --> 00:57:07,109
See that?

848
00:57:07,192 --> 00:57:08,527
What are you doing?

849
00:57:08,610 --> 00:57:11,071
I'm wiggling both my ears at the same time.

850
00:57:13,281 --> 00:57:15,075
That's it, smile.

851
00:57:17,744 --> 00:57:21,581
It took me two solid years at the best boys' school in the world to learn that.

852
00:57:21,623 --> 00:57:24,835
The fellow who taught me is now president of Venezuela.

853
00:57:26,795 --> 00:57:28,213
That's it!

854
00:57:32,009 --> 00:57:34,594
-Is it a giraffe? -No, not a giraffe.

855
00:57:34,678 --> 00:57:37,597
-l bet it is. -What?

856
00:57:37,639 --> 00:57:39,766
Well, then it's an elephant.

857
00:57:39,975 --> 00:57:43,687
- 是只公雞 - 公雞！
-It抯 supposed to be a rooster. -A rooster!

858
00:57:44,187 --> 00:57:48,025
You know an awful lot of tricks. Are you a professional magician?

859
00:57:48,108 --> 00:57:50,694
-No, I'm not a magician. -l was just joking.

860
00:57:50,777 --> 00:57:52,446
You really don't know who I am?

861
00:57:52,487 --> 00:57:55,616
You told me your name, Mr. Kane, but I'm awfully ignorant.

862
00:57:55,657 --> 00:58:00,162
I guess you caught on to that. I bet I've heard your name a million times.

863
00:58:00,329 --> 00:58:03,457
You really like me, though, even though you don't know who I am?

864
00:58:03,498 --> 00:58:06,960
I surely do. You've been wonderful.

865
00:58:08,045 --> 00:58:10,797
Without you I don't know what I would have done.

866
00:58:10,839 --> 00:58:14,176
I had a toothache, and I don't know many people.

867
00:58:14,509 --> 00:58:16,386
I know too many people.

868
00:58:16,845 --> 00:58:18,847
I guess we're both lonely.

869
00:58:19,181 --> 00:58:23,143
Want to know what I was going to do before I ruined my best Sunday clothes?

870
00:58:23,185 --> 00:58:26,480
I bet they're not your best Sunday clothes. You probably have more.

871
00:58:26,521 --> 00:58:27,731
I was just joking.

872
00:58:29,483 --> 00:58:33,403
I was on my way to the Western Manhattan Warehouse...

873
00:58:33,487 --> 00:58:35,489
...in search of my youth.

874
00:58:36,573 --> 00:58:41,203
You see, my mother died a long time ago. Her things were put in storage out West.

875
00:58:41,286 --> 00:58:43,747
There wasn't any other place to put them.

876
00:58:43,830 --> 00:58:47,876
I thought I'd send for them now. Tonight I was going to take a look at them.

877
00:58:47,960 --> 00:58:50,170
A sort of sentimental journey.

878
00:58:53,757 --> 00:58:56,093
I run a couple of newspapers. What do you do?

879
00:58:56,176 --> 00:58:57,177
Me?

880
00:58:57,469 --> 00:58:59,680
-How old did you say you were? -l didn't say.

881
00:58:59,763 --> 00:59:02,474
If you had, I wouldn't have asked you.

882
00:59:02,557 --> 00:59:03,809
-How old? -Pretty old.

883
00:59:03,892 --> 00:59:05,978
-How old? -Twenty-two in August.

884
00:59:06,061 --> 00:59:08,063
That's a ripe old age. What do you do?

885
00:59:08,146 --> 00:59:11,608
I work at Seligman's. I'm in charge of the sheet music.

886
00:59:11,900 --> 00:59:16,488
-Is that what you want to do? -No, I wanted to be a singer, I guess.

887
00:59:17,239 --> 00:59:20,826
-That is, I didn't. My mother did for me. -What happened to the singing?

888
00:59:20,909 --> 00:59:24,913
Mother always thought, she always talked about grand opera for me.

889
00:59:24,997 --> 00:59:26,206
Imagine.

890
00:59:26,498 --> 00:59:30,711
But my voice isn't that kind. It's just, you know what mothers are like.

891
00:59:32,504 --> 00:59:33,714
Yes.

892
00:59:35,757 --> 00:59:38,552
-Have you got a piano? -A piano?

893
00:59:39,553 --> 00:59:41,346
Yes, there's one in the parlor.

894
00:59:41,430 --> 00:59:44,474
-Would you sing for me? -You wouldn't want to hear me sing.

895
00:59:44,558 --> 00:59:45,934
Yes, I would.

896
00:59:47,728 --> 00:59:49,980
Don't tell me your toothache is bothering you.

897
00:59:50,063 --> 00:59:51,773
No, that's all gone.

898
00:59:53,525 --> 00:59:55,485
All right.

899
00:59:56,445 --> 00:59:58,238
Let's go to the parlor.

900
00:59:58,280 --> 01:00:04,119
"Yes, Lindor shall be mine

901
01:00:05,495 --> 01:00:09,041
I have sworn it
"l have sworn it

902
01:00:09,916 --> 01:00:12,753
For weal or woe
"For weal or woe

903
01:00:14,838 --> 01:00:17,591
"Yes, Lindor

904
01:00:24,348 --> 01:00:27,643
Lo giurai
"Lo giurai

905
01:00:29,061 --> 01:00:32,981
"La vincer?

906
01:00:40,989 --> 01:00:43,492
There is only one man who can rid the politics...

907
01:00:43,533 --> 01:00:46,828
...of this state of the evil domination of Boss Jim Gettys.

908
01:00:46,912 --> 01:00:50,832
I am speaking of Charles Foster Kane, the fighting liberal...

909
01:00:50,874 --> 01:00:54,461
...the friend of the workingman, the next governor of this state...

910
01:00:54,503 --> 01:00:56,797
...who entered upon this campaign....

911
01:00:56,838 --> 01:00:58,840
...with one purpose only:

912
01:00:58,882 --> 01:01:03,261
To point out and make public the dishonesty...

913
01:01:03,512 --> 01:01:08,308
...the downright villainy of Boss Jim W. Gettys' political machine...

914
01:01:08,600 --> 01:01:12,938
...now in complete control of the government of this state.

915
01:01:13,355 --> 01:01:17,150
I made no campaign promises...

916
01:01:17,651 --> 01:01:22,281
...because, until a few weeks ago, I had no hope of being elected.

917
01:01:23,907 --> 01:01:28,078
Now, however, l have something more than a hope.

918
01:01:31,331 --> 01:01:33,834
Jim Gettys has something less than a chance.

919
01:01:38,255 --> 01:01:40,173
Every straw vote...

920
01:01:40,340 --> 01:01:45,178
...every independent poll shows that l will be elected.

921
01:01:45,595 --> 01:01:47,306
Very well.

922
01:01:47,472 --> 01:01:50,225
Now l can afford to make some promises.

923
01:02:00,527 --> 01:02:02,696
The workingman and the slum child...

924
01:02:02,738 --> 01:02:07,909
...know they can expect my best efforts in their interests.

925
01:02:07,993 --> 01:02:12,539
The decent, ordinary citizens know that I?l do everything in my power...

926
01:02:12,623 --> 01:02:17,836
...to protect the underprivileged, the underpaid, and the underfed.

927
01:02:21,757 --> 01:02:26,136
-Mother, is Pop governor yet? -Not yet, Junior.

928
01:02:30,015 --> 01:02:33,393
I'd make my promises now...

929
01:02:33,685 --> 01:02:37,147
...if I weren't too busy arranging to keep them.

930
01:02:41,735 --> 01:02:44,738
But here's one promise I?l make...

931
01:02:44,821 --> 01:02:47,866
...and Boss Jim Gettys knows I?l keep it.

932
01:02:49,159 --> 01:02:51,995
My first official act as governor of this state...

933
01:02:52,412 --> 01:02:57,292
...will be to appoint a special district attorney to arrange for the indictment...

934
01:02:57,501 --> 01:03:03,215
...prosecution and conviction of Boss Jim W. Gettys.

935
01:03:29,449 --> 01:03:32,661
If the election were held today, you'd be in by 100,000 votes.

936
01:03:32,703 --> 01:03:34,579
-Gettys isn't even pretending. -Pop.

937
01:03:34,663 --> 01:03:36,790
-Hello, son. -He isn't just scared, he's sick.

938
01:03:36,873 --> 01:03:39,710
It's beginning to dawn on Jim Gettys I mean what I say.

939
01:03:39,793 --> 01:03:43,171
-Did you like your old man's speech? -l could hear every word.

940
01:03:43,213 --> 01:03:44,881
-Hello, Emily. -Hold it.

941
01:03:45,465 --> 01:03:48,135
-Great speech, Mr. Kane. -Wonderful.

942
01:03:48,176 --> 01:03:50,512
Will you get us a taxi?

943
01:03:51,888 --> 01:03:54,808
I'm sending Junior home in the car with Oliver.

944
01:03:55,183 --> 01:03:56,810
Good night, Father.

945
01:03:58,145 --> 01:04:00,022
Goodbye, son.

946
01:04:10,699 --> 01:04:14,161
Why did you send Junior home in the car? What are you doing in a taxi?

947
01:04:14,244 --> 01:04:16,413
There's a call I want you to make with me.

948
01:04:16,496 --> 01:04:18,415
-It can wait. -No, it can't.

949
01:04:18,498 --> 01:04:20,167
What's this all about, Emily?

950
01:04:20,250 --> 01:04:23,503
It may not be about anything at all. I intend to find out.

951
01:04:23,587 --> 01:04:27,674
-Where are you going? -I? going to "185 West 74th Street."

952
01:04:29,217 --> 01:04:32,137
If you wish, you may come with me.

953
01:04:36,308 --> 01:04:37,851
I'll come with you.

954
01:04:43,023 --> 01:04:46,485
I had no idea you had this flair for melodrama, Emily.

955
01:04:48,070 --> 01:04:50,072
Come right in, Mr. Kane.

956
01:05:00,624 --> 01:05:02,000
Charlie.

957
01:05:08,715 --> 01:05:11,802
He forced me to send your wife that letter. I didn't want to.

958
01:05:11,885 --> 01:05:14,680
He's been saying the most terrible....

959
01:05:18,976 --> 01:05:20,644
Mrs. Kane.

960
01:05:22,646 --> 01:05:25,148
I don't suppose anybody would introduce us.

961
01:05:26,608 --> 01:05:28,193
I'm Jim Gettys.

962
01:05:28,777 --> 01:05:29,945
Yes?

963
01:05:33,198 --> 01:05:36,952
I made Miss Alexander send you the note, Mrs. Kane.

964
01:05:36,994 --> 01:05:39,288
She didn't want to at first. But she did it.

965
01:05:39,329 --> 01:05:42,165
Charlie, the things he said to me.... He threatened to--

966
01:05:43,292 --> 01:05:47,129
I won't wait until I'm elected. To start with, I think I'll break your neck.

967
01:05:47,212 --> 01:05:49,965
Maybe you can do it, and maybe you can't.

968
01:05:51,049 --> 01:05:55,178
Your breaking this man's neck would scarcely explain this note:

969
01:05:56,138 --> 01:05:59,975
"Serious consequences for Mr. Kane, for yourself and for your son."

970
01:06:00,058 --> 01:06:03,103
-He wanted to get her to come here-- -What does this note mean?

971
01:06:03,145 --> 01:06:05,772
I'm Susan Alexander. I know what you're thinking--

972
01:06:05,814 --> 01:06:08,650
-What does this note mean, Ms. Alexander? -She don't know.

973
01:06:08,734 --> 01:06:11,570
She sent it because I told her it wouldn't be smart not to.

974
01:06:11,653 --> 01:06:14,948
-Emily, this gentleman-- -I? not a gentleman.

975
01:06:15,157 --> 01:06:18,035
Your husband is only trying to be funny calling me one.

976
01:06:18,118 --> 01:06:20,704
I don't even know what a gentleman is.

977
01:06:21,747 --> 01:06:25,000
You see, my idea of a gentleman....

978
01:06:28,170 --> 01:06:31,632
If I owned a paper and didn't like the way somebody was doing things...

979
01:06:31,673 --> 01:06:34,134
...some politician, I'd fight him with all I had.

980
01:06:34,176 --> 01:06:36,011
I wouldn't show him in a convict suit...

981
01:06:36,094 --> 01:06:38,472
...so his family could see his picture in the paper.

982
01:06:38,513 --> 01:06:42,476
-You're a cheap, crooked grafter-- -We're talking now about what you are.

983
01:06:43,393 --> 01:06:46,229
I'm fighting for my life, not just my political life.

984
01:06:46,313 --> 01:06:48,482
-He said unless you-- -That's what I said.

985
01:06:48,565 --> 01:06:52,569
Here's the chance I'm willing to give him. It's more of a chance than he'd give me.

986
01:06:52,653 --> 01:06:57,407
Unless he decides by tomorrow that he's so sick he has to go away for a year...

987
01:06:57,491 --> 01:07:01,787
...Monday morning, all papers in the state, except his, will carry the story I'll give.

988
01:07:01,870 --> 01:07:03,246
What story?

989
01:07:04,039 --> 01:07:07,209
-The story about him and Ms. Alexander. -There isn't any story!

990
01:07:07,292 --> 01:07:08,502
Shut up.

991
01:07:08,877 --> 01:07:11,713
We've got evidence that would look bad in the headlines.

992
01:07:11,755 --> 01:07:14,424
Do you want me to give you the evidence?

993
01:07:16,551 --> 01:07:19,388
I'd rather he withdrew without having the story published.

994
01:07:19,429 --> 01:07:22,516
Not that I care about him, but I'd be better off that way.

995
01:07:25,435 --> 01:07:27,521
So would you, Mrs. Kane.

996
01:07:27,938 --> 01:07:30,023
What about me?

997
01:07:32,025 --> 01:07:35,862
He said my name would be dragged through the mud. That everywhere I went--

998
01:07:35,904 --> 01:07:38,782
There seems to be only one decision you can make, Charles.

999
01:07:39,408 --> 01:07:41,576
I'd say that it'd been made for you.

1000
01:07:43,245 --> 01:07:45,330
You can't tell me the voters of this state--

1001
01:07:45,580 --> 01:07:48,375
I'm not interested in the voters of this state right now.

1002
01:07:48,417 --> 01:07:49,918
I am interested in our son.

1003
01:07:49,960 --> 01:07:53,130
-Charlie, if they publish this story-- -They won't.

1004
01:07:54,423 --> 01:07:56,550
Good night, Mr. Gettys.

1005
01:08:05,475 --> 01:08:07,561
Are you coming, Charles?

1006
01:08:09,813 --> 01:08:11,148
No.

1007
01:08:14,568 --> 01:08:16,194
I'm staying here.

1008
01:08:19,323 --> 01:08:21,408
I can fight this all alone.

1009
01:08:22,659 --> 01:08:25,162
If you don't listen to reason, it may be too late.

1010
01:08:25,245 --> 01:08:29,207
Too late? For what?

1011
01:08:31,293 --> 01:08:34,588
For you and this public thief...

1012
01:08:34,921 --> 01:08:37,257
...to take the love of the people away from me?

1013
01:08:37,299 --> 01:08:40,093
You got other things to think about. Your little boy.

1014
01:08:40,218 --> 01:08:42,679
You don't want him to read about you in the papers.

1015
01:08:42,763 --> 01:08:47,267
There's only one person in the world to decide what I'll do. And that's me.

1016
01:08:50,145 --> 01:08:53,690
You decided what you were going to do, Charles, some time ago.

1017
01:09:00,948 --> 01:09:03,617
You're making a bigger fool of yourself than I thought.

1018
01:09:03,659 --> 01:09:06,036
- 我跟你沒話可說 - 你輸了…
-I抳e got nothing to say to you. -You're licked--

1019
01:09:06,119 --> 01:09:09,498
Get out. If you want to see me, have the warden write me a letter.

1020
01:09:09,748 --> 01:09:14,419
If it was anybody else, I'd say what's going to happen to you would be a lesson to you.

1021
01:09:14,670 --> 01:09:17,381
Only you're going to need more than one lesson.

1022
01:09:18,215 --> 01:09:22,260
-And you'll get more than one lesson. -Don't worry about me, Gettys.

1023
01:09:23,929 --> 01:09:25,973
Don't worry about me!

1024
01:09:26,890 --> 01:09:29,476
I'm Charles Foster Kane!

1025
01:09:30,060 --> 01:09:34,356
I'm no cheap, crooked politician trying to save himself...

1026
01:09:34,398 --> 01:09:37,442
...from the consequences of his crimes!

1027
01:09:38,485 --> 01:09:42,489
Gettys! I'm gonna send you to Sing Sing.

1028
01:09:42,698 --> 01:09:44,825
Sing Sing, Gettys.

1029
01:09:48,036 --> 01:09:51,123
-Have you a car, Mrs. Kane? -Yes, thank you.

1030
01:09:51,665 --> 01:09:54,418
-Good night. -Good night.

1031
01:10:05,220 --> 01:10:09,182
Paper. Read all about it. Extra, extra.

1032
01:10:13,729 --> 01:10:15,814
-Paper? -No, thanks.

1033
01:10:30,704 --> 01:10:33,540
With a million majority already against him...

1034
01:10:33,790 --> 01:10:37,127
...and the church counties still to be heard from...

1035
01:10:37,336 --> 01:10:39,755
...I? afraid we got no choice.

1036
01:10:39,838 --> 01:10:41,256
This one?

1037
01:10:44,718 --> 01:10:46,470
That one.

1038
01:11:09,785 --> 01:11:11,620
Good night again.

1039
01:11:22,798 --> 01:11:26,259
-Is there anything I can-- -No, thanks, Mr. Bernstein.

1040
01:11:27,928 --> 01:11:30,222
You better go home and get some sleep.

1041
01:11:31,306 --> 01:11:32,766
You, too.

1042
01:11:35,185 --> 01:11:36,979
Good night, Mr. Kane.

1043
01:12:02,129 --> 01:12:04,673
-Hello, Jedediah. -I? drunk.

1044
01:12:08,343 --> 01:12:13,473
If you've got drunk to talk to me about...

1045
01:12:16,018 --> 01:12:18,562
...Ms. Alexander, don't bother.

1046
01:12:20,397 --> 01:12:22,190
I'm not interested.

1047
01:12:26,403 --> 01:12:30,282
I've set back the sacred cause of reform, is that it?

1048
01:12:31,742 --> 01:12:33,160
All right.

1049
01:12:34,911 --> 01:12:38,206
If that's the way they want it, the people have made their choice.

1050
01:12:38,290 --> 01:12:41,084
It's obvious the people prefer Jim Gettys to me.

1051
01:12:41,168 --> 01:12:44,838
You talk about the people as though you owned them.

1052
01:12:44,921 --> 01:12:47,215
As though they belong to you. Goodness.

1053
01:12:47,966 --> 01:12:52,763
As long as I can remember, you've talked about giving the people their rights...

1054
01:12:52,929 --> 01:12:56,183
...as if you could make them a present of liberty...

1055
01:12:56,308 --> 01:12:58,852
...as a reward for services rendered.

1056
01:12:58,894 --> 01:13:01,313
-Jed. -You remember the workingman?

1057
01:13:02,064 --> 01:13:04,691
I'll get drunk, too, Jedediah...

1058
01:13:06,193 --> 01:13:07,444
...if it'll do any good.

1059
01:13:07,527 --> 01:13:10,948
It won't do any good. Besides you never get drunk.

1060
01:13:11,073 --> 01:13:13,909
You used to write an awful lot about the workingman--

1061
01:13:13,951 --> 01:13:14,868
Go on home.

1062
01:13:14,910 --> 01:13:17,788
He's turning into something called "organized labor."

1063
01:13:19,581 --> 01:13:22,918
You won't like that one little bit when you find out...

1064
01:13:23,001 --> 01:13:28,131
...it means your workingman expects something as his right, and not your gift.

1065
01:13:30,217 --> 01:13:34,721
When your precious underprivileged really get together....

1066
01:13:35,305 --> 01:13:37,140
Oh, boy....

1067
01:13:37,933 --> 01:13:40,894
That'll add up to something bigger than your privilege...

1068
01:13:40,978 --> 01:13:43,939
...then I don't know what you'll do.

1069
01:13:43,981 --> 01:13:49,278
Sail away to a desert island probably and lord it over the monkeys.

1070
01:13:50,570 --> 01:13:52,739
I wouldn't worry about it too much.

1071
01:13:53,115 --> 01:13:57,995
There'll probably be a few of them there to tell me when I do something wrong.

1072
01:13:58,203 --> 01:14:00,289
You may not always be so lucky.

1073
01:14:01,123 --> 01:14:02,582
You're not very drunk.

1074
01:14:02,624 --> 01:14:04,668
Drunk, what do you care?

1075
01:14:05,085 --> 01:14:07,963
You don't care about anything except you.

1076
01:14:08,964 --> 01:14:14,803
You persuade people that you love them so much that they ought to love you back.

1077
01:14:15,137 --> 01:14:17,764
Only you want love on your own terms.

1078
01:14:19,474 --> 01:14:22,936
It's something to be played your way, according to your rules.

1079
01:14:29,568 --> 01:14:32,779
-Let me work on the Chicago paper. -What?

1080
01:14:33,071 --> 01:14:37,868
You said you were looking for someone to do dramatic "crimitism," criticism.

1081
01:14:38,118 --> 01:14:39,828
I am drunk.

1082
01:14:42,372 --> 01:14:44,374
I want to go to Chicago.

1083
01:14:45,667 --> 01:14:47,419
You're too valuable here.

1084
01:14:49,671 --> 01:14:53,634
-There's nothing left for me to do-- -All right, you can go to Chicago.

1085
01:14:53,717 --> 01:14:54,968
Thank you.

1086
01:14:56,053 --> 01:14:58,597
I guess I'd better try to get drunk anyway.

1087
01:15:00,307 --> 01:15:02,809
I warn you, Jedediah, you won't like Chicago.

1088
01:15:02,851 --> 01:15:07,481
The wind comes off the lake, and they've probably never heard of Lobster Newburg.

1089
01:15:07,564 --> 01:15:09,775
Will Saturday after next be all right?

1090
01:15:09,858 --> 01:15:11,818
-Anytime you say. -Thank you.

1091
01:15:17,532 --> 01:15:22,496
A toast to love on my terms. Those are the only terms anybody knows:

1092
01:15:24,122 --> 01:15:25,332
His own.

1093
01:15:38,261 --> 01:15:40,806
Mr. Kane, I'm from the Inquirer.

1094
01:15:50,065 --> 01:15:52,985
-What's that, young man? -Are you through with politics?

1095
01:15:53,068 --> 01:15:55,779
Am I through with politics? I would say vice versa.

1096
01:15:57,155 --> 01:15:58,865
We're going to be an opera star.

1097
01:15:58,907 --> 01:16:00,784
Are you singing at the Metropolitan?

1098
01:16:00,867 --> 01:16:02,244
We certainly are.

1099
01:16:02,411 --> 01:16:05,289
Charlie said if I didn't, he'd build me an opera house.

1100
01:16:05,372 --> 01:16:06,873
That won't be necessary.

1101
01:16:11,753 --> 01:16:15,048
No, no, no!

1102
01:16:48,624 --> 01:16:50,125
--
Places, please!

1103
01:17:39,216 --> 01:17:42,010
Mr. Leland is writing it from the dramatic angle?

1104
01:17:42,094 --> 01:17:45,555
-And we've covered it from the news end. -And the social.

1105
01:17:46,431 --> 01:17:48,642
How about the music notice?

1106
01:17:48,684 --> 01:17:50,686
Yes, it's already made up.

1107
01:17:50,769 --> 01:17:53,438
Mr. Mowan wrote a swell review.

1108
01:17:54,982 --> 01:17:59,152
-Enthusiastic? -Yes, sir.

1109
01:17:59,278 --> 01:18:01,905
Mr. Kane. This is a surprise.

1110
01:18:04,491 --> 01:18:06,493
We've got two spreads of pictures.

1111
01:18:06,535 --> 01:18:08,412
The music notice on the front page?

1112
01:18:08,495 --> 01:18:12,165
But there's still one notice to come. The dramatic.

1113
01:18:13,292 --> 01:18:14,835
The dramatic notice.

1114
01:18:16,003 --> 01:18:20,549
-Mr. Bernstein, that's Mr. Leland, isn't it? -Yes, we're waiting for it.

1115
01:18:24,428 --> 01:18:27,514
-Where is he? -Right in there, Mr. Kane.

1116
01:18:35,397 --> 01:18:36,898
Mr. Kane.

1117
01:18:51,705 --> 01:18:54,374
Mr. Leland and Mr. Kane...

1118
01:18:55,500 --> 01:18:58,754
...haven't spoken together for years.

1119
01:19:01,381 --> 01:19:04,051
-You don't suppose-- -There's nothing to suppose.

1120
01:19:08,430 --> 01:19:09,723
Excuse me.

1121
01:19:26,782 --> 01:19:28,283
Close the door.

1122
01:19:31,620 --> 01:19:34,164
He ain't been drinking before.

1123
01:19:35,040 --> 01:19:37,125
Never. We would have heard.

1124
01:19:37,459 --> 01:19:39,419
What does it say there?

1125
01:19:39,920 --> 01:19:42,589
The notice, what's he written?

1126
01:19:44,299 --> 01:19:49,262
"Miss Susan Alexander, a pretty but hopelessly incompetent amateur...

1127
01:19:52,808 --> 01:19:56,645
"...last night opened the new Chicago Opera House...

1128
01:19:56,979 --> 01:19:58,730
"...in a performance of...."

1129
01:20:01,817 --> 01:20:05,362
I still can't pronounce that name.

1130
01:20:08,865 --> 01:20:12,828
"Her singing, happily, is no concern of this department.

1131
01:20:13,245 --> 01:20:17,165
"Of her acting, it is absolutely impossible to...."

1132
01:20:18,875 --> 01:20:20,043
Go on.

1133
01:20:22,504 --> 01:20:24,506
That's all there is.

1134
01:20:37,602 --> 01:20:41,148
Of her acting it is absolutely impossible to say anything except...

1135
01:20:41,231 --> 01:20:44,818
...that, in the opinion of this reviewer, it represents a new low.

1136
01:20:45,027 --> 01:20:47,946
-In the opinion of this reviewer. -l didn't see that.

1137
01:20:48,030 --> 01:20:50,365
It isn't here, Mr. Bernstein, I'm dictating it.

1138
01:20:50,449 --> 01:20:53,368
-Mr. Kane, l-- -Get me a typewriter.

1139
01:20:54,828 --> 01:20:57,205
I'm going to finish Mr. Leland's notice.

1140
01:21:14,306 --> 01:21:16,224
Hello, Bernstein.

1141
01:21:21,355 --> 01:21:24,691
-Hello. -Hello, Mr. Leland.

1142
01:21:27,986 --> 01:21:31,198
Where's my notice, Bernstein? I've got to finish my notice.

1143
01:21:31,281 --> 01:21:34,534
-Mr. Kane is finishing it for you. -Charlie?

1144
01:21:40,540 --> 01:21:42,876
Charlie out there?

1145
01:21:44,628 --> 01:21:47,089
I guess he's fixing it up.

1146
01:21:49,508 --> 01:21:51,927
I knew I'd never get that through.

1147
01:21:52,761 --> 01:21:56,765
Mr. Kane's finishing your review just the way you started it.

1148
01:21:57,182 --> 01:22:00,894
He's writing a bad notice like you wanted it to be.

1149
01:22:03,397 --> 01:22:05,399
I guess that'll show you.

1150
01:22:30,299 --> 01:22:32,217
Hello, Jedediah.

1151
01:22:35,470 --> 01:22:36,930
Hello, Charlie.

1152
01:22:38,515 --> 01:22:41,351
I didn't know we were speaking.

1153
01:22:41,685 --> 01:22:43,937
Sure we're speaking, Jedediah.

1154
01:22:45,981 --> 01:22:48,150
You're fired.

1155
01:23:00,454 --> 01:23:05,000
Everybody knows that story, Mr. Leland, but why did he do it?

1156
01:23:06,501 --> 01:23:09,671
-How could a man write a notice-- -You just don't know Charlie.

1157
01:23:09,755 --> 01:23:13,675
He thought that by finishing that notice he'd show me he was an honest man.

1158
01:23:13,759 --> 01:23:16,011
He was always trying to prove something.

1159
01:23:16,094 --> 01:23:19,222
That whole thing about Susie being an opera singer.

1160
01:23:19,306 --> 01:23:21,558
That was trying to prove something.

1161
01:23:22,351 --> 01:23:25,687
You know what the headline was the day before the election?

1162
01:23:26,063 --> 01:23:32,069
"Candidate Kane found in love nest with 'singer."'

1163
01:23:32,152 --> 01:23:34,279
He was going to take the quotes off the singer.

1164
01:23:34,363 --> 01:23:35,489
Hey, nurse!

1165
01:23:35,572 --> 01:23:39,576
Five years ago he wrote from that place down there in the South.

1166
01:23:39,660 --> 01:23:47,334
What's it called, Shangri-la? EI Dorado? Sloppy Joe's? What's the name?

1167
01:23:48,543 --> 01:23:52,881
All right, Xanadu, I knew it all the time.

1168
01:23:52,965 --> 01:23:57,094
You caught on, didn't you? I'm not that hard to see through.

1169
01:23:57,511 --> 01:23:59,888
Well, I never even answered his letter.

1170
01:24:00,681 --> 01:24:02,057
Maybe I should have.

1171
01:24:02,140 --> 01:24:06,186
I guess he was pretty lonely down there in that coliseum all those years.

1172
01:24:06,645 --> 01:24:09,731
He hadn't finished it when she left him. He never finished it.

1173
01:24:09,815 --> 01:24:13,026
He never finished anything except my notice.

1174
01:24:13,402 --> 01:24:16,571
-Of course, he built the joint for her. -That must have been love.

1175
01:24:16,655 --> 01:24:17,614
I don't know.

1176
01:24:17,864 --> 01:24:22,369
He was disappointed in the world so he built his own, an absolute monarchy.

1177
01:24:23,078 --> 01:24:25,664
It was something bigger than an opera house anyway.

1178
01:24:25,747 --> 01:24:27,124
-Nurse. -Yes, Mr. Leland.

1179
01:24:27,207 --> 01:24:28,583
I'm coming.

1180
01:24:30,127 --> 01:24:33,630
-There's one thing you can do for me. -Sure.

1181
01:24:33,755 --> 01:24:38,385
Stop at the cigar store on your way out, and get me a couple of good cigars.

1182
01:24:38,468 --> 01:24:40,554
-Be glad to. -Thank you.

1183
01:24:41,054 --> 01:24:42,472
One is enough.

1184
01:24:42,848 --> 01:24:47,269
When I was a young man, there was an impression that nurses were pretty.

1185
01:24:47,311 --> 01:24:49,396
Well, it was no truer then than it is today.

1186
01:24:49,479 --> 01:24:51,607
-I?l take your arm. -All right.

1187
01:24:51,690 --> 01:24:54,401
-You won't forget about those cigars? -l won't.

1188
01:24:54,610 --> 01:24:59,573
Have them wrapped like toothpaste, or they'll stop them at the desk.

1189
01:24:59,615 --> 01:25:02,534
You know that young doctor I was telling you about, well...

1190
01:25:02,618 --> 01:25:06,496
...he's got an idea he wants to keep me alive.

1191
01:25:35,776 --> 01:25:39,780
I'd rather you'd just talk. Anything that comes into your mind...

1192
01:25:40,489 --> 01:25:42,699
...about yourself and Mr. Kane.

1193
01:25:42,908 --> 01:25:48,246
You don't want to hear what comes into my mind about myself and Charlie Kane.

1194
01:25:49,790 --> 01:25:54,586
You know, maybe I shouldn't have sung for Charlie that first time I met him.

1195
01:25:55,837 --> 01:25:58,757
But I did an awful lot of singing after that.

1196
01:26:00,634 --> 01:26:04,513
I sang for teachers at $100 an hour.

1197
01:26:04,554 --> 01:26:07,140
-The teachers got that, I didn't. -What did you get?

1198
01:26:07,224 --> 01:26:10,811
I didn't get a thing, except music lessons. That's all there was in it.

1199
01:26:10,852 --> 01:26:12,354
He married you, didn't he?

1200
01:26:12,396 --> 01:26:15,857
He didn't mention anything about marriage until after it was over and...

1201
01:26:15,941 --> 01:26:18,277
...until it got in the papers about us...

1202
01:26:18,360 --> 01:26:23,240
...and he lost the election, and that Norton woman divorced him.

1203
01:26:23,573 --> 01:26:25,617
He was really interested in my voice.

1204
01:26:25,701 --> 01:26:27,286
Why did he build that opera house?

1205
01:26:27,369 --> 01:26:31,206
I didn't want it. I didn't want a thing. It was his idea.

1206
01:26:32,582 --> 01:26:34,668
Everything was his idea...

1207
01:26:37,629 --> 01:26:39,631
...except my leaving him.

1208
01:26:47,597 --> 01:26:52,269
?
"Don't forget"

1209
01:26:52,352 --> 01:26:53,854
Don't get nervous.

1210
01:26:53,979 --> 01:26:56,648
Please. Let's come back.

1211
01:26:58,233 --> 01:27:01,445
Look at me, Mrs. Kane, darling.

1212
01:27:01,570 --> 01:27:10,495
"Get the voice out of the throat Place the tone right in the mask

1213
01:27:16,710 --> 01:27:18,045
"Diaphragm"

1214
01:27:25,552 --> 01:27:27,846
You're out of pitch.

1215
01:27:31,600 --> 01:27:34,686
Some people can sing. Some can't.

1216
01:27:34,853 --> 01:27:36,396
Impossible.

1217
01:27:37,314 --> 01:27:40,400
It's not your job to give your opinion of Mrs. Kane's talents.

1218
01:27:42,319 --> 01:27:45,906
You're supposed to train her voice, Signor Matiste. Nothing more.

1219
01:27:46,615 --> 01:27:48,408
Please continue with the lesson.

1220
01:27:48,450 --> 01:27:50,911
-But, Mr. Kane. -Please.

1221
01:27:51,912 --> 01:27:55,791
I'll be the laughingstock of the musical world. People will think--

1222
01:27:55,832 --> 01:27:58,794
You're concerned what people will think?

1223
01:27:58,835 --> 01:28:03,423
Perhaps I can enlighten you a bit. I'm an authority on what people will think.

1224
01:28:04,132 --> 01:28:05,926
The newspapers for example.

1225
01:28:06,176 --> 01:28:08,929
I run several newspapers between here and San Francisco.

1226
01:28:08,971 --> 01:28:10,430
It's all right, darling.

1227
01:28:10,639 --> 01:28:12,891
Signor Matiste is going to listen to reason.

1228
01:28:13,809 --> 01:28:16,561
-How can I persuade you-- -You can't.

1229
01:28:42,796 --> 01:28:44,923
It's all right, darling, go ahead.

1230
01:28:56,685 --> 01:28:59,438
I thought you'd see it my way.

1231
01:29:09,698 --> 01:29:13,619
No, no, no.

1232
01:29:43,899 --> 01:29:47,402
?
"Ah, cruel

1233
01:29:48,737 --> 01:29:52,199
?
"Tu m'as trop entendue

1234
01:29:53,367 --> 01:29:56,870
?
"Les Dieux m'en sont t?oins

1235
01:29:57,329 --> 01:30:01,750
?
"Ces Dieux qui dans mon flanc

1236
01:30:01,875 --> 01:30:04,169
?
"Ont allum?

1237
01:30:04,294 --> 01:30:07,005
?
"Le feu fatal

1238
01:30:07,422 --> 01:30:10,467
?
"A tout mon sang

1239
01:30:30,445 --> 01:30:35,409
?
"Dites-moi comment j'expie

1240
01:30:35,450 --> 01:30:38,704
?

1241
01:30:40,789 --> 01:30:43,750
?
"Toujours remplie

1242
01:30:43,834 --> 01:30:48,839
?

1243
01:30:52,259 --> 01:30:56,930
?
"Oh Dieux, arrachez-moi

1244
01:30:57,222 --> 01:30:59,641
?
"Ces feux fatals"

1245
01:30:59,725 --> 01:31:00,809
I think it's dreadful.

1246
01:31:01,393 --> 01:31:05,939
?
"Allument ma mort

1247
01:31:10,694 --> 01:31:15,407
?
"Voil?mon coeur

1248
01:31:15,991 --> 01:31:20,829
?
"Voil?mon coeur

1249
01:31:21,330 --> 01:31:25,334
?
"C'est l?que ta main doit frapper

1250
01:31:27,336 --> 01:31:30,297
?
"Voil?mon coeur

1251
01:31:32,341 --> 01:31:34,635
?
"Frappe

1252
01:31:35,844 --> 01:31:40,307
?

1253
01:31:41,433 --> 01:31:43,810
?
"Frappe"

1254
01:32:40,409 --> 01:32:42,828
Stop telling me he's your friend.

1255
01:32:43,245 --> 01:32:45,664
A friend don't write that kind of review.

1256
01:32:45,747 --> 01:32:49,167
All these other papers panning me, I could expect that.

1257
01:32:49,251 --> 01:32:53,046
But for the Inquirer to run a thing like that, spoiling my whole debut.

1258
01:32:53,088 --> 01:32:53,880
Come in.

1259
01:32:53,922 --> 01:32:54,881
I'll get it.

1260
01:32:54,965 --> 01:32:57,801
Friend. Not the kind of friends I know...

1261
01:32:58,093 --> 01:33:00,095
...but I'm not high class like you.

1262
01:33:00,345 --> 01:33:02,389
And I never went to any swell schools.

1263
01:33:02,472 --> 01:33:03,932
That'll be enough, Susan.

1264
01:33:03,974 --> 01:33:05,225
From Mr. Leland, sir.

1265
01:33:05,767 --> 01:33:08,895
He wanted me to make sure you got this personally.

1266
01:33:09,187 --> 01:33:11,815
Is that something from him?

1267
01:33:13,275 --> 01:33:14,484
Charlie!

1268
01:33:15,277 --> 01:33:19,114
As for you, you ought to have your head examined.

1269
01:33:19,323 --> 01:33:21,908
Sending him a letter telling him he's fired...

1270
01:33:21,950 --> 01:33:24,453
...with a $25,000 check in it.

1271
01:33:24,536 --> 01:33:27,080
What kind of firing do you call that?

1272
01:33:27,748 --> 01:33:32,002
You did send him a check for $25,000, didn't you?

1273
01:33:33,295 --> 01:33:34,296
Yes.

1274
01:33:36,798 --> 01:33:40,010
I sent him a check for $25,000.

1275
01:33:42,638 --> 01:33:44,097
What's that?

1276
01:33:45,307 --> 01:33:46,808
Declaration of Principles.

1277
01:33:46,892 --> 01:33:47,976
What?

1278
01:33:48,685 --> 01:33:49,770
What is it?

1279
01:33:55,609 --> 01:33:56,610
An antique.

1280
01:33:56,693 --> 01:33:59,446
You're awful funny, aren't you?

1281
01:34:00,530 --> 01:34:04,826
I'll tell you one thing you're not going to be funny about, and that's my singing.

1282
01:34:04,910 --> 01:34:06,036
I'm through.

1283
01:34:06,119 --> 01:34:08,622
I never wanted to do it in the first place.

1284
01:34:09,164 --> 01:34:11,667
You'll continue with your singing, Susan.

1285
01:34:11,833 --> 01:34:14,628
I don't propose to have myself made ridiculous.

1286
01:34:14,670 --> 01:34:17,965
You don't propose to have yourself made ridiculous!

1287
01:34:18,048 --> 01:34:21,385
What about me? I'm the one that has to sing.

1288
01:34:21,468 --> 01:34:23,428
I'm the one that gets the raspberries.

1289
01:34:23,512 --> 01:34:25,347
Why don't you leave me alone?

1290
01:34:25,430 --> 01:34:27,140
My reasons satisfy me, Susan.

1291
01:34:27,224 --> 01:34:29,351
You seem unable to understand them.

1292
01:34:30,227 --> 01:34:32,479
I will not tell them to you again.

1293
01:34:36,149 --> 01:34:38,318
You'll continue with your singing.

1294
01:36:10,786 --> 01:36:12,621
Get Dr. Corey.

1295
01:36:17,042 --> 01:36:18,126
Susan.

1296
01:36:19,002 --> 01:36:22,631
She'll be perfectly all right in a day or two, Mr. Kane.

1297
01:36:24,633 --> 01:36:28,679
I can't imagine how Mrs. Kane came to make such a foolish mistake.

1298
01:36:29,179 --> 01:36:33,767
The sedative Dr. Wagner gave her was in a somewhat larger bottle.

1299
01:36:34,142 --> 01:36:38,605
The strain of preparing for the new opera has excited and confused her.

1300
01:36:38,689 --> 01:36:40,941
Yes, I'm sure that was it.

1301
01:36:41,733 --> 01:36:44,486
No objections to my staying with her are there?

1302
01:36:44,653 --> 01:36:48,740
No, not at all. I'd like the nurse to be here, too.

1303
01:36:49,533 --> 01:36:51,660
Good night, Mr. Kane.

1304
01:37:27,195 --> 01:37:28,822
Charlie.

1305
01:37:31,533 --> 01:37:34,202
I couldn't make you see how I felt, Charlie.

1306
01:37:36,163 --> 01:37:38,915
But I couldn't go through with the singing again.

1307
01:37:40,834 --> 01:37:44,046
You don't know what it means to know that people....

1308
01:37:45,547 --> 01:37:48,342
That the whole audience doesn't want you.

1309
01:37:51,970 --> 01:37:54,056
That's when you got to fight them.

1310
01:38:02,481 --> 01:38:04,024
All right.

1311
01:38:05,984 --> 01:38:08,403
You won't have to fight them anymore.

1312
01:38:11,698 --> 01:38:13,825
It's their loss.

1313
01:38:29,466 --> 01:38:31,218
What are you doing?

1314
01:38:35,681 --> 01:38:37,683
Jigsaw puzzles?

1315
01:38:42,813 --> 01:38:44,815
Charlie, what time is it?

1316
01:38:45,399 --> 01:38:46,900
-11?30
-11:30.

1317
01:38:47,067 --> 01:38:48,485
In New York?

1318
01:38:51,405 --> 01:38:54,241
I said what time is it in New York?

1319
01:38:55,075 --> 01:38:58,203
-11 :30. -Night?

1320
01:39:01,790 --> 01:39:03,625
The Bulldog's just gone to press.

1321
01:39:03,709 --> 01:39:06,920
Well, hurray for the Bulldog.

1322
01:39:08,755 --> 01:39:12,551
Gee, 11:30. Shows are just getting up.

1323
01:39:12,676 --> 01:39:15,429
People are going to nightclubs and restaurants.

1324
01:39:16,096 --> 01:39:19,474
Of course, we're different because we live in a palace.

1325
01:39:19,558 --> 01:39:22,185
You always said you wanted to live in a palace.

1326
01:39:22,269 --> 01:39:24,646
A person could go crazy in this dump.

1327
01:39:24,813 --> 01:39:28,650
Nobody to talk to, nobody to have any fun with.

1328
01:39:29,401 --> 01:39:33,280
-49,000 acres of nothing but scenery and statues.

1329
01:39:33,488 --> 01:39:34,990
I'm lonesome.

1330
01:39:35,032 --> 01:39:38,994
Until yesterday, we've had no less than 50 of your friends at any one time.

1331
01:39:39,036 --> 01:39:41,788
I think if you look in the west wing...

1332
01:39:41,830 --> 01:39:44,958
...you'll probably find a dozen vacationists still in residence.

1333
01:39:45,000 --> 01:39:47,628
You make a joke out of everything.

1334
01:39:48,128 --> 01:39:52,758
Charlie, I want to go to New York. I'm tired of being a hostess.

1335
01:39:52,841 --> 01:39:56,678
I want to have fun. Please, Charlie.

1336
01:39:58,388 --> 01:40:01,224
Charlie, please.

1337
01:40:02,184 --> 01:40:04,811
Our home is here, Susan.

1338
01:40:10,567 --> 01:40:12,819
I don't care to visit New York.

1339
01:40:49,731 --> 01:40:51,525
What are you doing?

1340
01:40:54,486 --> 01:40:57,781
One thing I can never understand:

1341
01:40:57,864 --> 01:41:00,033
How do you know you haven't done it before?

1342
01:41:00,117 --> 01:41:04,121
It makes a whole lot more sense than collecting statues.

1343
01:41:05,747 --> 01:41:07,332
You may be right.

1344
01:41:07,749 --> 01:41:09,835
I sometimes wonder...

1345
01:41:10,210 --> 01:41:12,629
...but you get into the habit.

1346
01:41:12,713 --> 01:41:15,590
It's not a habit, I do it because I like it.

1347
01:41:16,008 --> 01:41:18,593
I thought we might have a picnic tomorrow.

1348
01:41:20,971 --> 01:41:24,016
Invite everybody to spend the night at the Everglades.

1349
01:41:24,266 --> 01:41:28,645
Invite everybody. Order everybody, you mean, and make them sleep in tents.

1350
01:41:28,729 --> 01:41:31,732
Who wants to sleep in tents when they've got their own room...

1351
01:41:31,773 --> 01:41:34,401
...with a bath, where they know where everything is?

1352
01:41:34,484 --> 01:41:37,904
I thought we might have a picnic tomorrow.

1353
01:41:41,241 --> 01:41:45,579
You never give me anything I really care about.

1354
01:41:54,129 --> 01:41:56,006
"It can't be love

1355
01:41:58,508 --> 01:42:03,138
"For there is no true love

1356
01:42:03,513 --> 01:42:06,016
"l know I've played at the game

1357
01:42:06,099 --> 01:42:08,977
"Like a moth in a blue flame

1358
01:42:09,019 --> 01:42:12,856
"Lost in the end, just the same

1359
01:42:12,940 --> 01:42:15,442
"All these years

1360
01:42:15,609 --> 01:42:20,489
"My heart's been floating around in a puddle of tears

1361
01:42:22,908 --> 01:42:24,868
"l wonder what it is"

1362
01:42:24,952 --> 01:42:28,997
Sure, you give me things, but that don't mean anything to you.

1363
01:42:31,416 --> 01:42:34,503
You're in a tent, darling, you're not at home.

1364
01:42:34,586 --> 01:42:37,965
I can hear you very well if you speak in a normal tone of voice.

1365
01:42:38,006 --> 01:42:41,969
What's the difference between giving me a bracelet or giving someone $100,000...

1366
01:42:42,010 --> 01:42:45,055
...for a statue you'll keep crated up and never look at?

1367
01:42:45,138 --> 01:42:47,516
It's just money. It doesn't mean anything.

1368
01:42:47,599 --> 01:42:50,811
You never really gave me anything that you care about.

1369
01:42:50,894 --> 01:42:52,688
I want you to stop this.

1370
01:42:52,771 --> 01:42:54,189
I'm not going to stop it.

1371
01:42:54,273 --> 01:42:56,566
You never gave me anything in your life.

1372
01:42:56,650 --> 01:42:59,945
You just tried to buy me into giving you something.

1373
01:43:02,698 --> 01:43:06,618
"It can't be love"

1374
01:43:19,464 --> 01:43:22,050
Whatever I do, I do because I love you.

1375
01:43:22,134 --> 01:43:24,094
You don't love me.

1376
01:43:24,219 --> 01:43:26,179
You want me to love you.

1377
01:43:26,221 --> 01:43:29,182
Sure. "I? Charles Foster Kane.

1378
01:43:29,224 --> 01:43:31,852
"Whatever you want, just name it and it's yours.

1379
01:43:31,935 --> 01:43:34,187
"But you gotta love me."

1380
01:43:38,650 --> 01:43:41,361
Don't tell me you're sorry.

1381
01:43:43,322 --> 01:43:45,073
I'm not sorry.

1382
01:43:56,918 --> 01:43:58,503
Mr. Kane?

1383
01:43:58,920 --> 01:44:01,673
Mrs. Kane would like to see you, sir.

1384
01:44:04,551 --> 01:44:07,721
Marie has been packing for her since morning.

1385
01:44:13,810 --> 01:44:17,522
Tell Arnold I'm ready, Marie. Tell him he can get the bags.

1386
01:44:18,065 --> 01:44:19,316
Yes, madame.

1387
01:44:21,318 --> 01:44:23,695
Have you gone completely crazy?

1388
01:44:23,779 --> 01:44:27,032
Don't you know that our guests, that everyone will know about this?

1389
01:44:27,115 --> 01:44:28,951
Packed your bag, sent for the car--

1390
01:44:29,034 --> 01:44:30,077
And left you?

1391
01:44:30,369 --> 01:44:32,287
Of course they'll hear.

1392
01:44:33,288 --> 01:44:35,958
I'm not saying goodbye, except to you.

1393
01:44:36,166 --> 01:44:39,002
But I never imagined people wouldn't know.

1394
01:44:41,463 --> 01:44:43,340
I won't let you go.

1395
01:44:50,681 --> 01:44:52,307
Goodbye, Charlie.

1396
01:44:55,894 --> 01:44:57,312
Susan.

1397
01:45:07,489 --> 01:45:09,116
Please don't go.

1398
01:45:14,746 --> 01:45:16,540
Please, Susan.

1399
01:45:19,751 --> 01:45:23,213
From now on, everything will be exactly the way you want it to be.

1400
01:45:23,297 --> 01:45:25,424
Not the way I think you want it, but...

1401
01:45:27,092 --> 01:45:28,593
...your way.

1402
01:45:35,100 --> 01:45:36,435
You mustn't go.

1403
01:45:39,688 --> 01:45:41,815
You can't do this to me.

1404
01:45:43,525 --> 01:45:45,193
I see.

1405
01:45:46,111 --> 01:45:48,655
It's you that this is being done to.

1406
01:45:48,989 --> 01:45:50,782
It's not me at all.

1407
01:45:51,033 --> 01:45:52,951
Not what it means to me.

1408
01:45:55,120 --> 01:45:57,289
I can't do this to you?

1409
01:45:58,749 --> 01:46:00,709
Oh, yes, I can.

1410
01:46:19,853 --> 01:46:22,981
In case you haven't heard, I lost all my money and it was plenty.

1411
01:46:23,065 --> 01:46:25,359
The last 10 years have been difficult for many.

1412
01:46:25,442 --> 01:46:28,403
They haven't been tough on me. I just lost all my money.

1413
01:46:29,029 --> 01:46:32,199
-You're going down to Xanadu? -Monday, with boys from the office.

1414
01:46:32,240 --> 01:46:35,035
Mr. Rawlston wants the whole place photographed.

1415
01:46:35,077 --> 01:46:36,703
We run a picture magazine.

1416
01:46:36,745 --> 01:46:40,499
If you're smart, you'll get in touch with Raymond. He's the butler.

1417
01:46:40,582 --> 01:46:42,542
You'll learn a lot from him.

1418
01:46:42,626 --> 01:46:45,128
He knows where all the bodies are buried.

1419
01:46:45,295 --> 01:46:48,507
You know, all the same, I feel kind of sorry for Mr. Kane.

1420
01:46:50,759 --> 01:46:52,427
Don't you think I do?

1421
01:46:57,015 --> 01:46:59,977
What do you know, it's morning already.

1422
01:47:03,438 --> 01:47:07,067
Come around and tell me the story of your life sometime.

1423
01:47:14,074 --> 01:47:15,659
Rosebud?

1424
01:47:18,287 --> 01:47:20,372
I'll tell you about Rosebud.

1425
01:47:20,872 --> 01:47:22,791
How much is it worth to you?

1426
01:47:23,375 --> 01:47:25,168
$1,000?

1427
01:47:34,469 --> 01:47:37,180
Well, I'll tell you, Mr. Thompson.

1428
01:47:37,889 --> 01:47:41,310
-He acted funny sometimes, you know? -No, I didn't.

1429
01:47:41,560 --> 01:47:43,979
Yes, he did crazy things sometimes.

1430
01:47:44,313 --> 01:47:46,732
I've been working for him 11 years now...

1431
01:47:46,815 --> 01:47:50,611
...in charge of the whole place, so I ought to know.

1432
01:47:50,986 --> 01:47:52,279
-Rosebud. -Yes.

1433
01:47:52,321 --> 01:47:57,326
Like I tell you, the old man acted kind of funny sometimes...

1434
01:47:57,409 --> 01:48:01,121
-...but I knew how to handle him-- -Need a lot of service?

1435
01:48:01,204 --> 01:48:04,249
But I knew how to handle him.

1436
01:48:04,374 --> 01:48:06,084
Like that time his wife left.

1437
01:50:21,803 --> 01:50:23,055
Rosebud.

1438
01:51:31,123 --> 01:51:32,666
I see.

1439
01:51:33,333 --> 01:51:35,794
And that's what you know about Rosebud?

1440
01:51:37,337 --> 01:51:39,715
I heard him say it that other time, too.

1441
01:51:40,382 --> 01:51:42,301
He just said:

1442
01:51:42,676 --> 01:51:44,511
"Rosebud."

1443
01:51:44,553 --> 01:51:47,848
Then he dropped the glass ball and it broke on the floor.

1444
01:51:48,557 --> 01:51:52,769
He didn't say anything after that, and I knew he was dead.

1445
01:51:53,520 --> 01:51:56,231
He said all kinds of things that didn't mean anything.

1446
01:51:56,315 --> 01:51:58,317
Sentimental fellow, aren't you?

1447
01:51:59,401 --> 01:52:02,070
-Yes and no. -That isn't worth $1,000.

1448
01:52:02,154 --> 01:52:05,157
You can keep on asking questions if you want to.

1449
01:52:05,407 --> 01:52:07,075
We're leaving tonight...

1450
01:52:07,868 --> 01:52:09,828
...when we're through taking pictures.

1451
01:52:09,911 --> 01:52:11,872
Allow yourself plenty of time.

1452
01:52:11,913 --> 01:52:15,792
The train stops at the junction on signal, but they don't like to wait.

1453
01:52:16,835 --> 01:52:19,796
I can remember when they'd wait all day...

1454
01:52:20,130 --> 01:52:21,840
...if Mr. Kane said so.

1455
01:52:23,258 --> 01:52:25,844
Better get going.

1456
01:52:28,847 --> 01:52:32,768
-Can we come down? -Yes, hurry up. We're leaving.

1457
01:52:34,978 --> 01:52:38,440
How much do you think this is all worth, Mr. Thompson?

1458
01:52:38,649 --> 01:52:40,192
Millions.

1459
01:52:41,652 --> 01:52:42,861
If anybody wants it.

1460
01:52:42,945 --> 01:52:45,614
Well, at least he brought all this stuff to America.

1461
01:52:45,697 --> 01:52:47,449
-What's that? -Another Venus.

1462
01:52:47,532 --> 01:52:48,867
$25,000.

1463
01:52:48,951 --> 01:52:51,536
A lot of money to pay for a dame without a head.

1464
01:52:51,620 --> 01:52:53,789
The banks are out of luck?

1465
01:52:55,290 --> 01:52:58,043
-They'll clear all right. -He never threw anything away.

1466
01:52:58,126 --> 01:53:03,298
"Welcome home, Mr. Kane, from 467 employees of the New York Inquirer."

1467
01:53:03,382 --> 01:53:08,762
"One stove from the estate of Mary Kane, Little Salem, Colorado. Value: $2."

1468
01:53:08,804 --> 01:53:11,306
We're supposed to get everything, junk as well as art.

1469
01:53:11,390 --> 01:53:13,100
He sure liked to collect things.

1470
01:53:13,141 --> 01:53:14,559
Anything and everything.

1471
01:53:14,643 --> 01:53:16,353
A regular crow, eh?

1472
01:53:16,436 --> 01:53:19,147
-Hey, look, a jigsaw puzzle. -We got a lot of those.

1473
01:53:19,231 --> 01:53:22,276
A Burmese temple and three Spanish ceilings down the hall.

1474
01:53:22,776 --> 01:53:24,236
Part of a Scotch castle...

1475
01:53:24,319 --> 01:53:26,113
...that needs to be unwrapped.

1476
01:53:26,488 --> 01:53:27,948
Put all this stuff together:

1477
01:53:28,031 --> 01:53:31,159
The palaces and the paintings, and the toys and everything.

1478
01:53:31,243 --> 01:53:33,245
What would it spell?

1479
01:53:34,663 --> 01:53:37,457
-Charles Foster Kane? -Or Rosebud.

1480
01:53:37,708 --> 01:53:40,961
-How about it, Jerry? -What's Rosebud?

1481
01:53:41,044 --> 01:53:43,297
That's what he said when he died.

1482
01:53:43,964 --> 01:53:46,133
Did you ever find out what it means?

1483
01:53:46,174 --> 01:53:48,427
-No, I didn't. -What did you find out about him?

1484
01:53:48,510 --> 01:53:49,970
Not much, really.

1485
01:53:53,098 --> 01:53:54,808
We'd better get started.

1486
01:53:56,727 --> 01:53:59,146
What have you been doing all this time?

1487
01:54:00,188 --> 01:54:01,815
Playing with a jigsaw puzzle.

1488
01:54:01,857 --> 01:54:05,652
If you'd discovered what Rosebud meant, I bet it would've explained everything.

1489
01:54:05,694 --> 01:54:07,279
No, I don't think so.

1490
01:54:07,904 --> 01:54:09,323
No.

1491
01:54:09,448 --> 01:54:12,993
He was a man who got everything he wanted, and then lost it.

1492
01:54:13,118 --> 01:54:16,830
Maybe Rosebud was something he couldn't get or something he lost.

1493
01:54:16,872 --> 01:54:19,166
It wouldn't have explained anything.

1494
01:54:20,042 --> 01:54:23,170
I don't think any word can explain a man's life.

1495
01:54:24,129 --> 01:54:28,342
No. I guess Rosebud is just a piece in a jigsaw puzzle.

1496
01:54:29,509 --> 01:54:31,219
A missing piece.

1497
01:54:37,059 --> 01:54:39,227
Come on, everybody...

1498
01:54:41,063 --> 01:54:43,315
...we'll miss the train.

1499
01:55:48,005 --> 01:55:50,257
Throw that junk in.

1500
01:57:21,853 --> 01:57:25,657
後面自己翻譯的不好，請見諒

1501
01:57:26,853 --> 01:57:30,857
Maybe I was what you nowadays call a stooge.

1502
01:57:31,483 --> 01:57:33,485
Everything was his idea...

1503
01:57:35,445 --> 01:57:37,447
...except my leaving him.

1504
01:57:38,365 --> 01:57:40,367
I've got his trunk all packed.

1505
01:57:41,660 --> 01:57:43,829
I've had it packed for a week now.

1506
01:57:44,079 --> 01:57:47,541
Sometimes I think I'd prefer a rival of flesh and blood.

1507
01:57:49,251 --> 01:57:51,753
You're gonna need more than one lesson.

1508
01:57:52,921 --> 01:57:55,382
And you'll get more than one lesson.

1509
01:58:00,679 --> 01:58:02,180
Who's a busy man, me?

1510
01:58:02,431 --> 01:58:05,726
I'm chairman of the board. I got nothing but time.

1511
01:58:05,934 --> 01:58:07,352
What do you want to know?

1512
01:58:07,436 --> 01:58:12,065
We thought.... If we could find out what he meant by his last words as he was dying....

1513
01:58:12,566 --> 01:58:14,735
Sentimental fellow, aren't you?

1514
01:58:16,111 --> 01:58:17,237
Yes and no.

1515
01:58:17,613 --> 01:58:20,407
"l think it would be fun to run a newspaper."
